250
00:16:14,113 --> 00:16:16,530
and that it was self-defence.

251
00:16:16,553 --> 00:16:18,912
He agrees that he wasn't very well
at the time.

252
00:16:18,913 --> 00:16:20,912
Then why have you accepted
the prosecution's offer

253
00:16:20,913 --> 00:16:22,432
of guilty to manslaughter,

254
00:16:22,433 --> 00:16:25,392
instead of running him
not guilty to murder?

255
00:16:25,393 --> 00:16:28,112
You don't really want me to answer that?

256
00:16:28,113 --> 00:16:30,697
I'll pretend I'm not listening.

257
00:16:31,073 --> 00:16:34,907
Is this man well enough in mind
to stand trial?

258
00:16:34,993 --> 00:16:37,192
The psychiatric report
suggests he now is.

259
00:16:37,193 --> 00:16:39,312
He started to eat his victim.

260
00:16:39,313 --> 00:16:41,472
guilty due to
diminished responsibility

261
00:16:41,473 --> 00:16:43,792
is the simplest way
to deal with him, John.

262
00:16:43,793 --> 00:16:47,072
Yeah, but is it the fairest? I would
like to hear some evidence in this case.

263
00:16:47,073 --> 00:16:50,072
- From whom?
- From the defendant, for a start.

264
00:16:50,073 --> 00:16:51,792
I mean,
is this diminished responsibility,

265
00:16:51,793 --> 00:16:55,460
is it provocation
or is it self-defence? Hmm?

266
00:16:56,913 --> 00:16:58,912
- Do you want to run him not guilty?
- I'm not inclined to.

267
00:16:58,913 --> 00:17:01,232
All right. We'll go
guilty to manslaughter.

268
00:17:01,233 --> 00:17:04,072
But in view of the contradictions,
I want to examine the evidence.

269
00:17:04,073 --> 00:17:07,392
Well, in that case, my side might well
want a jury to tr the murder.

270
00:17:07,393 --> 00:17:11,352
I mean, you can't run some sort of
Newton hearing in a murder case!

271
00:17:11,353 --> 00:17:12,992
I thought he was pleading
to manslaughter.

272
00:17:12,993 --> 00:17:14,592
- John.
- Look, can either of you

273
00:17:14,593 --> 00:17:17,352
direct me to the law that says I can't?

274
00:17:17,353 --> 00:17:18,866
Can you?

275
00:17:18,953 --> 00:17:23,152
A jury could convict him of murder
or it could acquit him of everything.

276
00:17:23,153 --> 00:17:26,672
Now, it's far better if we agree that
it's one or another kind of manslaughter

277
00:17:26,673 --> 00:17:28,512
and I examine which it is.

278
00:17:28,513 --> 00:17:31,592
That's how 800 years
of case law evolved,

279
00:17:31,593 --> 00:17:35,343
by a judge doing something
for the first time.

280
00:17:35,753 --> 00:17:40,592
We'd like to make a statement.
You running? Okay. Here we go.

281
00:17:40,593 --> 00:17:42,872
This is the kind of hearing
that's being run.

282
00:17:42,873 --> 00:17:45,752
No jury and no justice for Paul Settle.

283
00:17:45,753 --> 00:17:48,670
No jury, no justice for my brother.

284
00:17:57,193 --> 00:18:00,512
Why do I have to go over it
again and again?

285
00:18:00,513 --> 00:18:03,013
It undermines the Home Office.

286
00:18:03,073 --> 00:18:05,872
- I won't allow it.
- Regrettable, Deed's done it.

287
00:18:05,873 --> 00:18:08,952
I'm not sure there's anything
you or I can do about it.

288
00:18:08,953 --> 00:18:10,272
We could sack him.

289
00:18:10,273 --> 00:18:12,992
That would be very nice,
Home Secretary.

290
00:18:12,993 --> 00:18:14,592
However, as a High Court judge,

291
00:18:14,593 --> 00:18:18,512
he is the very embodiment of the Crown
wherever he sits.

292
00:18:18,513 --> 00:18:20,097
He can't be sacked.

293
00:18:20,273 --> 00:18:24,232
Margaret. Have Sir Percy
liaise with Sir Ian.

294
00:18:25,113 --> 00:18:28,872
The judiciary's unbridled power
cannot continue.

295
00:18:28,873 --> 00:18:31,632
I want everyone in the Home Office
and Constitutional Affairs

296
00:18:31,633 --> 00:18:34,712
who have an opinion on that
to meet at their earliest convenience.

297
00:18:34,713 --> 00:18:35,748
Okay.

298
00:18:36,553 --> 00:18:39,053
We will have some action, Ian.

299
00:18:40,433 --> 00:18:45,017
The last thing I want to do
is defend the Deed, darling.

300
00:18:45,593 --> 00:18:48,952
But isn't there a risk of this
being seen as personal?

301
00:18:48,953 --> 00:18:50,752
It is personal, George.

302
00:18:50,753 --> 00:18:53,503
Deed finds an means to get at me.

303
00:18:53,793 --> 00:18:55,749
You're being paranoid.

304
00:18:55,833 --> 00:18:57,712
It's just a case he's running.

305
00:18:57,713 --> 00:18:59,792
George, it was open and shut.

306
00:18:59,793 --> 00:19:02,832
The defendant was being allowed
to plead guilty.

307
00:19:02,833 --> 00:19:05,352
Now Deed is running a hearing
like an examining magistrate.

308
00:19:05,353 --> 00:19:07,832
It allows him to ask
whatever questions he chooses

309
00:19:07,833 --> 00:19:10,552
about allegations of racism
in the Prison Service.

310
00:19:10,553 --> 00:19:11,872
All to embarrass me.

311
00:19:11,873 --> 00:19:14,912
Neil, he's got the two silks to agree.

312
00:19:14,913 --> 00:19:19,543
An interdepartmental meeting
about Deed alone is so OTT.

313
00:19:26,393 --> 00:19:29,592
- Do you want some dinner?
- Hmm. Are you eating alone?

314
00:19:29,593 --> 00:19:32,952
- You've been talking to Charlie.
- Ah! No little Miss Oxfam?

315
00:19:32,953 --> 00:19:35,552
No, she's in the middle of a trial.

316
00:19:35,553 --> 00:19:40,232
Charlie said Jo's boyfriend
in South Africa might be returning.

317
00:19:40,233 --> 00:19:42,150
This is social, George.

318
00:19:42,953 --> 00:19:45,537
Oh. You could offer me a drink.

319
00:19:56,353 --> 00:19:59,392
- Are you trying to embarrass Neil?
- How?

320
00:19:59,393 --> 00:20:01,977
With this bizarre Newton trial.

321
00:20:02,113 --> 00:20:07,112
He's called a conference of department heads to curb your excesses.

322
00:20:07,113 --> 00:20:10,712
Mmm. It's not your career
that concerns me, John.

323
00:20:10,713 --> 00:20:13,792
I don't want Neil embarrassing himself
by going after you.

324
00:20:13,793 --> 00:20:18,152
I don't think embarrassment's
in his emotional cannon, George.

325
00:20:18,153 --> 00:20:22,312
You don't care about anyone's feelings
so long as you can hold forth.

326
00:20:22,313 --> 00:20:25,392
- Pathological.
- I'm simply trying to do what is just.

327
00:20:25,393 --> 00:20:29,977
- You might be wrong.
- Why are you really here, George?

328
00:20:33,993 --> 00:20:37,152
Did you have evidence
of anything corrupt

329
00:20:37,153 --> 00:20:40,903
between Neil and his friend
Sir Tim Listfield?

330
00:20:41,793 --> 00:20:46,032
A politician is in a position
to help a sleazy arms dealer,

331
00:20:46,033 --> 00:20:48,752
and the arms dealer
is read to grease palms.

332
00:20:48,753 --> 00:20:51,253
How can there not be anything?

333
00:20:52,193 --> 00:20:55,152
For someone whose entire life
is predicated on fact,

334
00:20:55,153 --> 00:20:57,986
that is extraordinary conjecture.

335
00:20:58,073 --> 00:21:03,073
If there had been an evidence
I would have acted, wouldn't I?

336
00:21:06,113 --> 00:21:08,712
There was doubt, George.
And it's in you, too.

337
00:21:08,713 --> 00:21:10,463
It's why you're here.

338
00:21:11,113 --> 00:21:15,152
You must proceed according to
our own conscience, not mine.

339
00:21:15,153 --> 00:21:17,352
Do you know what I dislike
most about you, John?

340
00:21:17,353 --> 00:21:22,520
Your superiority, your perpetual claim
to the moral high ground.

341
00:21:22,673 --> 00:21:26,007
That's what I most dislike about myself.

342
00:21:35,193 --> 00:21:38,152
I couldn't believe what I saw
when I opened the cell.

343
00:21:38,153 --> 00:21:41,232
I have to admit to being scared,
in case Bradwell attacked me.

344
00:21:41,233 --> 00:21:44,232
Then I thought,
"That's sill. I'm white."

345
00:21:44,233 --> 00:21:46,392
You knew Bradwell was racist?

346
00:21:46,393 --> 00:21:48,592
Well, yes, it's in his book.

347
00:21:48,593 --> 00:21:50,632
What is this book, Mr Elkington?

348
00:21:50,633 --> 00:21:52,066
His file.

349
00:21:52,153 --> 00:21:54,672
It has to be read
by all officers coming on duty.

350
00:21:54,673 --> 00:21:56,392
Did you read it that night?

351
00:21:56,393 --> 00:21:58,953
No. I mean, I had before.

352
00:21:59,033 --> 00:22:02,264
And what did it say about Bradwell?

353
00:22:02,353 --> 00:22:04,752
Well, the usual stuff, I'm not sure.

354
00:22:04,753 --> 00:22:07,587
- But you have read it?
- Yes, sir.

355
00:22:07,833 --> 00:22:10,832
Do we have a cop of
Mr Bradwell's prison book in court?

356
00:22:10,833 --> 00:22:14,872
We do, my lord.
It's copied in your bundle. 15C.

357
00:22:20,513 --> 00:22:24,904
Says here, "Mr Bradwell is a racist

358
00:22:24,993 --> 00:22:29,872
"who thinks nothing of his personal
safety in pursuit of racist attacks.

359
00:22:29,873 --> 00:22:33,623
"No black officer must escort him
on his own."

360
00:22:33,913 --> 00:22:36,913
Yet you put a black man in his cell.

361
00:22:37,393 --> 00:22:39,952
There was a lot going on.
The dead man was acting up.

362
00:22:39,953 --> 00:22:43,432
Did you put him in with Bradwell
to punish him?

363
00:22:43,433 --> 00:22:47,267
He was causing trouble
and we were overcrowded.

364
00:22:48,233 --> 00:22:49,382
Okay.

365
00:22:50,033 --> 00:22:53,367
Mrs Mills, do you wish to cross-examine?

366
00:22:54,513 --> 00:22:56,872
Can you tell us your impression
of Mr Bradwell

367
00:22:56,873 --> 00:22:58,672
when you first opened the cell door?

368
00:22:58,673 --> 00:23:02,143
I saw Settle first. I almost threw up.

369
00:23:03,153 --> 00:23:07,403
Then I saw Bradwell.
He was sitting there, grinning.

370
00:23:07,913 --> 00:23:10,312
He had blood round his mouth.

371
00:23:10,313 --> 00:23:16,032
Then I twigged there were chunks of
flesh, what he'd bitten out of Paul.

372
00:23:16,033 --> 00:23:18,512
What was Mr Bradwell's manner?

373
00:23:18,513 --> 00:23:22,432
Calm. He was grinning.
Like he'd done something terrific.

374
00:23:22,433 --> 00:23:24,112
- Did you speak to him?
- No.

375
00:23:24,113 --> 00:23:26,872
I just wanted to get out of there
as fast as I could.

376
00:23:26,873 --> 00:23:28,873
Thank you, Mr Elkington.

377
00:23:30,073 --> 00:23:33,407
Mr Mason Allen,
do you have an questions?

378
00:23:34,233 --> 00:23:38,146
Um, what time did you find Mr Settle?

379
00:23:38,633 --> 00:23:41,912
Quarter to six. I punched
the rounds clock about 10 to.

380
00:23:41,913 --> 00:23:46,472
Mmm-hmm. What number was
Mr Bradwell's cell on your round?

381
00:23:46,473 --> 00:23:48,592
The third. The second one was empty.

382
00:23:48,593 --> 00:23:51,232
Mr Elkington, earlier you said
the prison was over capacity.

383
00:23:51,233 --> 00:23:54,650
Now you're saying
there was an empty cell.

384
00:23:55,393 --> 00:23:58,465
- Just one?
- Um, I'm not sure.

385
00:23:58,553 --> 00:24:01,192
There was a lot of confusion
on the wing.

386
00:24:01,193 --> 00:24:03,792
Well, was the prison full or not?

387
00:24:03,793 --> 00:24:08,293
Yes, sir. The empty cell was damaged.
It wasn't in use.

388
00:24:09,793 --> 00:24:12,023
I see. Mason Allen?

389
00:24:12,833 --> 00:24:15,152
I have no further questions.

390
00:24:15,153 --> 00:24:17,512
Thank you, Mr Elkington.
You can step down,

391
00:24:17,513 --> 00:24:23,592
but please stay in the vicinity of the
court, as you might be called again.

392
00:24:23,593 --> 00:24:26,752
I'd like to hear forensic evidence next.
Is the pathologist present in court?

393
00:24:26,753 --> 00:24:28,420
Yes, he is, my lord.

394
00:24:29,233 --> 00:24:33,067
Death occurred between
11 pm and 12 midnight.

395
00:24:33,153 --> 00:24:35,432
Mr Settle died from loss of blood,

396
00:24:35,433 --> 00:24:38,432
following a series
of stab wounds to the back.

397
00:24:38,433 --> 00:24:43,350
One punctured his left lung,
another the vena cava superior.

398
00:24:43,713 --> 00:24:46,912
How quickly do you estimate
death came, Dr Graceman?

399
00:24:46,913 --> 00:24:49,512
He would have survived
no more than 10 minutes.

400
00:24:49,513 --> 00:24:52,072
And would he have been
able to move or Cr out?

401
00:24:52,073 --> 00:24:54,632
It would have been
increasingly difficult.

402
00:24:54,633 --> 00:24:56,942
This was a fit 24-ear-old.

403
00:24:57,033 --> 00:25:00,352
It takes a lot to stop the life force
of such a person.

404
00:25:00,353 --> 00:25:05,592
- How man stab wounds were there?
- Three. The third was superficial.

405
00:25:05,593 --> 00:25:08,112
And did you draw an conclusions?

406
00:25:08,113 --> 00:25:10,312
Only that there was a struggle.

407
00:25:10,313 --> 00:25:14,730
The bruising on Paul Settle's torso
indicated as much.

408
00:25:16,513 --> 00:25:19,752
Could the stabbing have been in self-defence?

409
00:25:19,753 --> 00:25:22,313
I didn't examine the defendant

410
00:25:22,393 --> 00:25:26,352
and I haven't seen an medical report
following his arrest.

411
00:25:26,353 --> 00:25:28,770
I can't give a clear opinion.

412
00:25:29,193 --> 00:25:31,192
Is there a medical report?

413
00:25:31,193 --> 00:25:34,432
Only one that was made 24 hours
after the death of his cellmate.

414
00:25:34,433 --> 00:25:37,312
Mr Bradwell was examined
prior to seeing a psychiatrist.

415
00:25:37,313 --> 00:25:40,032
Did that report indicate

416
00:25:40,113 --> 00:25:43,152
that the defendant might have been
in a fight with the dead man?

417
00:25:43,153 --> 00:25:47,672
There were a number of cuts
and contusions. Some were fresh.

418
00:25:47,673 --> 00:25:50,752
The defendant has a history
of attacking people,

419
00:25:50,753 --> 00:25:53,952
some of whom, inevitably,
are more able to defend themselves.

420
00:25:53,953 --> 00:25:56,537
Yes, thank you, Mr Mason Allen.

421
00:25:57,553 --> 00:25:59,312
Dr Graceman,
can you tell the court about

422
00:25:59,313 --> 00:26:02,112
the bite marks on Paul Settle's bod?

423
00:26:02,113 --> 00:26:06,552
The bite was forensically matched
to the defendant's teeth pattern.

424
00:26:06,553 --> 00:26:09,152
Indeed, there were traces of saliva
around the wounds

425
00:26:09,153 --> 00:26:11,312
that matched Mr Bradwell's DNA.

426
00:26:11,313 --> 00:26:15,752
So there's no doubt that
Bradwell ate Paul Settle's flesh?

427
00:26:15,753 --> 00:26:18,792
None was found in the cell
to indicate he spat it out.

428
00:26:18,793 --> 00:26:23,232
Remember, the biting took place
up to four hours after the killing.

429
00:26:23,233 --> 00:26:26,712
- And how do you know this?
- Blood coagulation.

430
00:26:26,713 --> 00:26:29,713
The edges of the wound didn't bleed.

431
00:26:34,393 --> 00:26:37,749
He waited before eating his flesh?

432
00:26:39,953 --> 00:26:43,392
Mrs Settle, you might be better off
waiting outside.

433
00:26:43,393 --> 00:26:44,621
No, no.

434
00:26:45,713 --> 00:26:48,963
I want to hear what the say
about my boy.

435
00:26:50,113 --> 00:26:53,030
Very well. An questions, Mrs Mills?

436
00:26:55,793 --> 00:27:00,512
Have you an previous experience of
someone killing and eating their victim?

437
00:27:00,513 --> 00:27:02,552
Not personally. It's bizarre.

438
00:27:02,553 --> 00:27:04,312
Would you say it was insane?

439
00:27:04,313 --> 00:27:06,472
I'd have to conclude that.

440
00:27:06,473 --> 00:27:08,390
Thank you, Dr Graceman.

441
00:27:12,633 --> 00:27:14,032
Dr Graceman,

442
00:27:14,913 --> 00:27:20,112
are there not tribes
both in South America and Borneo

443
00:27:20,193 --> 00:27:24,027
who eat their victims
as a victor celebration?

444
00:27:24,113 --> 00:27:26,672
I'm a clinician, sir,
not an anthropologist.

445
00:27:26,673 --> 00:27:28,832
Mr Mason Allen,
was there an known history

446
00:27:28,833 --> 00:27:30,752
between Mr Settle and the defendant?

447
00:27:30,753 --> 00:27:32,587
Not that I'm aware of.

448
00:27:34,073 --> 00:27:38,589
Mr Bradwell has 18 previous
convictions for violence,

449
00:27:38,673 --> 00:27:41,272
mostly within prison institutions.

450
00:27:41,273 --> 00:27:44,106
And Paul Settle

451
00:27:44,193 --> 00:27:47,472
was convicted for assaulting
a police officer with a knife.

452
00:27:47,473 --> 00:27:49,752
No, he didn't! He was innocent.

453
00:27:49,753 --> 00:27:53,032
A belief you are entitled to pursue,
Mrs Settle, but not here.

454
00:27:53,033 --> 00:27:55,792
If I'm not allowed to
speak for Paul, who will?

455
00:27:55,793 --> 00:28:00,752
Mr De Mel, I understand that you are holding a watching brief for Mrs Settle.

456
00:28:00,753 --> 00:28:02,752
Now, whilst I am sympathetic
to your client,

457
00:28:02,753 --> 00:28:04,992
if she interrupts again
I will hold her in contempt.

458
00:28:04,993 --> 00:28:08,432
Mrs Settle is under tremendous strain.
She's barely kept her sanity

459
00:28:08,433 --> 00:28:10,632
as she's struggled to get to
the truth of what happened.

460
00:28:10,633 --> 00:28:14,392
I was not inviting your comments.
Just do as I ask.

461
00:28:14,393 --> 00:28:16,592
The policeman lied about Paul.

462
00:28:16,593 --> 00:28:20,432
The jury in your son's trial
took a different view.

463
00:28:20,433 --> 00:28:22,310
Now, please, sit down.

464
00:28:27,913 --> 00:28:30,163
Why'd you take Bradwell on?

465
00:28:31,033 --> 00:28:34,072
I'm a barrister. I subscribe
to the cab-rank principle.

466
00:28:34,073 --> 00:28:37,872
- Is this man worth your effort?
- What about your effort?

467
00:28:37,873 --> 00:28:41,512
Mont Everard seems to think
there could be a hidden agenda.

468
00:28:41,513 --> 00:28:43,185
Did he say whose?

469
00:28:44,553 --> 00:28:46,632
I'm sure Newman Mason Allen
thinks the same.

470
00:28:46,633 --> 00:28:48,050
Did he say whose?

471
00:28:50,753 --> 00:28:53,632
Ah, you're probably right
to examine this case as you are.

472
00:28:53,633 --> 00:28:56,192
Bradwell is as much of a victim
as the dead man.

473
00:28:56,193 --> 00:28:58,343
Is he? He ain't dead, Jo.

474
00:28:58,433 --> 00:29:00,752
He suffered abuse and neglect
as a child.

475
00:29:00,753 --> 00:29:04,232
Well, lots of people had a difficult
start in life. The overcome it.

476
00:29:04,233 --> 00:29:07,832
He's 29 years old
and has spent 24 of those years

477
00:29:07,833 --> 00:29:09,872
in some sort of institution or another.

478
00:29:09,873 --> 00:29:12,392
Yes, it's an appalling indictment
of the system.

479
00:29:12,393 --> 00:29:15,832
But the problem and the solution
both lie with Bradwell.

480
00:29:15,833 --> 00:29:18,352
John, this is a young man
full of pain and rage.

481
00:29:18,353 --> 00:29:20,232
One who is easily manipulated,

482
00:29:20,233 --> 00:29:23,432
as you can tell from his
attachment to the BNP or worse.

483
00:29:23,433 --> 00:29:27,912
And others like him didn't stab
and then eat their victims.

484
00:29:27,913 --> 00:29:31,112
Isn't it odd that someone who hates
the very breath black people take

485
00:29:31,113 --> 00:29:35,030
should do that to the victim, having killed him?

486
00:29:35,313 --> 00:29:36,952
So what are you saying?

487
00:29:36,953 --> 00:29:41,512
The psychiatrist thinks he might have been hearing schizoid voices.

488
00:29:41,513 --> 00:29:44,680
Bradwell said "the" told him to do it.

489
00:29:48,593 --> 00:29:50,948
Gotta go.

490
00:29:53,353 --> 00:29:54,945
Can we have dinner?

491
00:29:55,033 --> 00:29:57,617
- Not tonight, John.
- Jo, uh...

492
00:29:59,913 --> 00:30:02,072
Have I missed something here?

493
00:30:02,073 --> 00:30:05,072
I'm just trying to sort out
my feelings towards you.

494
00:30:05,073 --> 00:30:08,573
Okay, does that prevent me
from seeing you?

495
00:30:09,593 --> 00:30:12,927
Whenever I'm with you, I just seem to...

496
00:30:14,513 --> 00:30:16,513
...fall under your spell.

497
00:30:17,273 --> 00:30:21,752
Seeing you in court's bad enough,
seeing you socially means I...

498
00:30:21,753 --> 00:30:23,837
I don't know what I feel.

499
00:30:25,673 --> 00:30:28,631
Yeah. You heard from Michael?

500
00:30:29,113 --> 00:30:31,786
Yeah, he's doing well at school.

501
00:30:33,553 --> 00:30:36,992
Settling in all right, is he,
with his dad and his brothers?

502
00:30:36,993 --> 00:30:38,993
- Yeah, of course.
- Hmm.

503
00:30:44,193 --> 00:30:47,192
My feelings for you have remained
absolutely constant!

504
00:30:47,193 --> 00:30:48,752
That's more than could be said
for your actions.

505
00:30:48,753 --> 00:30:50,752
Well, what do you want me to do,
go back into therapy?

506
00:30:50,753 --> 00:30:54,337
Are you missing
sleeping with your therapist?

507
00:30:56,353 --> 00:30:58,187
Well, I deserved that.

508
00:31:13,993 --> 00:31:17,032
Mr Spearson, as the Principal Officer,

509
00:31:17,033 --> 00:31:20,352
can you tell the court what was happening on A-wing

510
00:31:20,353 --> 00:31:22,792
on the night Paul Settle was killed?

511
00:31:22,793 --> 00:31:24,704
Near total chaos, sir.

512
00:31:24,793 --> 00:31:28,832
Prisoners screaming,
banging their doors, flooding toilets.

513
00:31:28,833 --> 00:31:31,112
One even tried to start a fire.

514
00:31:31,113 --> 00:31:32,872
What was the cause of this?

515
00:31:32,873 --> 00:31:35,592
It's never one single thing, sir.

516
00:31:35,593 --> 00:31:38,072
There'd been an upset
earlier in the day,

517
00:31:38,073 --> 00:31:39,992
a prisoner tried to kill himself.

518
00:31:39,993 --> 00:31:42,752
That makes a lot of them
feel vulnerable.

519
00:31:42,753 --> 00:31:44,352
Including Paul Settle?

520
00:31:44,353 --> 00:31:47,151
Yes, sir. He'd been acting up.

521
00:31:47,273 --> 00:31:49,792
Is that why he was on a secure wing?

522
00:31:49,793 --> 00:31:51,293
I don't know, sir.

523
00:31:51,953 --> 00:31:53,632
Well, he was due for release shortly,
wasn't he?

524
00:31:53,633 --> 00:31:56,912
Aren't such prisoners
put on a less restricted regime?

525
00:31:56,913 --> 00:31:58,790
When the behave, sir.

526
00:31:58,873 --> 00:32:01,152
And Settle hadn't been behaving?

527
00:32:01,153 --> 00:32:02,552
No, sir.

528
00:32:02,633 --> 00:32:04,712
That's why he was serving
ever day of his sentence.

529
00:32:04,713 --> 00:32:06,432
That's because you lot
kept picking on him.

530
00:32:06,433 --> 00:32:07,752
Mrs Settle, I've warned you.

531
00:32:07,753 --> 00:32:10,352
Paul told me all about you, ow you were out to get him

532
00:32:10,353 --> 00:32:12,152
- and all the other black prisoners.
- Usher! Usher!

533
00:32:12,153 --> 00:32:15,653
Remove Mrs Settle,
don't let her come back.

534
00:32:22,593 --> 00:32:26,792
Mr Spearson, do you know why
Mr Settle was acting as he was?

535
00:32:26,793 --> 00:32:29,752
I think he was trying to convince the
world he hadn't done what he did, sir.

536
00:32:29,753 --> 00:32:33,712
Why was he put in a cell
with a known racist?

537
00:32:34,153 --> 00:32:37,872
- It shouldn't have happened, sir.
- But it did, and on your watch.

538
00:32:37,873 --> 00:32:40,952
The inquiry couldn't find out
why it happened, sir.

539
00:32:40,953 --> 00:32:42,537
It was human error.

540
00:32:42,673 --> 00:32:44,712
In the chaos, the guards
yanked out the ring leader

541
00:32:44,713 --> 00:32:47,463
and put him in another cell, sir.

542
00:32:47,473 --> 00:32:50,306
In a white supremacist's cell.

543
00:32:52,313 --> 00:32:56,647
The should have read the book, sir,
but the panicked.

544
00:32:57,233 --> 00:32:58,507
I see.

545
00:32:58,593 --> 00:33:02,752
Are protocols laid down
for prisoners such as Bradwell?

546
00:33:02,753 --> 00:33:05,072
Yes, sir. And the are
strictly adhered to.

547
00:33:05,073 --> 00:33:08,990
Not this time, Mr Spearson,
and it proved fatal.

548
00:33:10,633 --> 00:33:12,032
Mrs Mills?

549
00:33:13,593 --> 00:33:17,871
Mr Spearson, you gave the order
to move Paul Settle.

550
00:33:17,953 --> 00:33:20,232
- Yes, miss.
- Did you say where to?

551
00:33:20,233 --> 00:33:21,832
I said to the empty cell.

552
00:33:21,833 --> 00:33:23,512
I didn't know it was
out of action at the time.

553
00:33:23,513 --> 00:33:28,312
It is your job to know ever person
and ever function on the wing?

554
00:33:28,313 --> 00:33:31,908
Yes, miss. And I messed up, big time.

555
00:33:34,113 --> 00:33:37,697
Did you know Bradwell
would react as he did?

556
00:33:38,753 --> 00:33:41,152
No one could imagine he'd do that, miss.

557
00:33:41,153 --> 00:33:43,512
The internal inquiry
reprimanded Mr Faker,

558
00:33:43,513 --> 00:33:48,112
an inexperienced prison officer,
and Mr Elkington for their oversight,

559
00:33:48,113 --> 00:33:52,712
but didn't you have Paul Settle
placed in the defendant's cell

560
00:33:52,713 --> 00:33:55,152
to get back at him for acting up?

561
00:33:55,153 --> 00:33:56,825
No, of course not.

562
00:34:00,553 --> 00:34:02,512
But that is what happened.

563
00:34:02,513 --> 00:34:03,992
No, it wasn't.

564
00:34:04,393 --> 00:34:07,592
Do you have evidence
to support this, Mrs Mills?

565
00:34:07,593 --> 00:34:10,672
Well, no. But it is
a common sense inference.

566
00:34:10,673 --> 00:34:14,007
Then have you finished
with this witness?

567
00:34:14,273 --> 00:34:15,773
- Yes.
- Yes?

568
00:34:17,313 --> 00:34:18,826
Mr Mason Allen.

569
00:34:21,553 --> 00:34:23,748
Mr Spearson, um...

570
00:34:26,313 --> 00:34:30,232
What does the prison book say
about the defendant?

571
00:34:30,233 --> 00:34:36,733
He is a psychopathic racist who attacks
ethnic minorities without warning, sir.

572
00:34:39,033 --> 00:34:41,192
Has he made such attacks before?

573
00:34:41,193 --> 00:34:43,343
He has, frequently.

574
00:34:44,273 --> 00:34:46,190
Thank you, Mr Spearson.

575
00:34:46,913 --> 00:34:49,747
Mr Spearson, don't step away.

576
00:34:55,673 --> 00:34:58,992
What was your relationship
with the defendant?

577
00:34:58,993 --> 00:35:01,077
He was in my charge, sir.

578
00:35:01,833 --> 00:35:04,872
- Did he give you much trouble?
- No, sir.

579
00:35:04,873 --> 00:35:08,072
Despite everything, he was a lot less
trouble than the man he killed.

580
00:35:08,073 --> 00:35:09,752
Settle fermented a lot of unrest.

581
00:35:09,753 --> 00:35:12,352
You would have been pleased
to see the back of him?

582
00:35:12,353 --> 00:35:16,632
His release would have been
a break for everyone, sir.

583
00:35:16,633 --> 00:35:20,626
On the plan of the wing,
Mr Settle was...

584
00:35:21,233 --> 00:35:24,669
eight cells away from Bradwell. Correct?

585
00:35:24,753 --> 00:35:26,072
Yes, sir.

586
00:35:26,153 --> 00:35:28,272
Bradwell was in A-3, Settle in A-11.

587
00:35:28,273 --> 00:35:30,273
Who was in A-1, 2 and 4?

588
00:35:31,913 --> 00:35:33,585
I'm not sure, sir.

589
00:35:34,753 --> 00:35:37,170
Did A-1 have a broken toilet?

590
00:35:43,353 --> 00:35:44,945
I think so, sir.

591
00:35:49,033 --> 00:35:53,533
Thank you, Mr Spearson.
Don't leave the court precinct.

592
00:35:56,513 --> 00:35:59,992
Mr Mason Allen, I think you were
intending to call two prisoners,

593
00:35:59,993 --> 00:36:02,512
are either of them still available?

594
00:36:02,513 --> 00:36:04,272
I stood both down, my lord,

595
00:36:04,273 --> 00:36:07,512
when my learned friend for the defence
and I agreed a plea.

596
00:36:07,513 --> 00:36:08,912
Oh, I see.

597
00:36:08,993 --> 00:36:10,752
Well, then I'd first like to take a view

598
00:36:10,753 --> 00:36:12,912
of the scene of the killing.
Mr Harrison?

599
00:36:12,913 --> 00:36:15,072
Certainly, my lord.
When would you like to go?

600
00:36:15,073 --> 00:36:17,348
After lunch? All right.

601
00:36:17,433 --> 00:36:19,232
Your client should be present,
Mrs Mills,

602
00:36:19,233 --> 00:36:22,150
and Mr De Mel, as there is no jury.

603
00:36:27,273 --> 00:36:30,232
Mr Baggs, you were
the Duty Assistant Governor

604
00:36:30,233 --> 00:36:31,632
on the night Paul Settle was killed?

605
00:36:31,633 --> 00:36:33,232
Yes, sir. The eight to eight shift.

606
00:36:33,233 --> 00:36:35,512
- Twelve hours?
- There's a rest provision, sir.

607
00:36:35,513 --> 00:36:37,832
We do four 12-hour shifts
and have three days off.

608
00:36:37,833 --> 00:36:40,192
Did you sleep the night
Paul Settle was killed?

609
00:36:40,193 --> 00:36:42,552
- No, sir, there was too much trouble.
- Hmm.

610
00:36:42,553 --> 00:36:44,392
Was it your decision to move him?

611
00:36:44,393 --> 00:36:47,312
Mr Spearson has control of the wing.

612
00:36:47,313 --> 00:36:51,312
Yes, but did you approve the decision
to move him into the cell

613
00:36:51,313 --> 00:36:54,063
with a prisoner who was a racist?

614
00:36:54,273 --> 00:36:57,832
No, sir, putting him
into Bradwell's cell didn't arise.

615
00:36:57,833 --> 00:37:00,912
Well, did you warn them not to do it?

616
00:37:00,913 --> 00:37:03,952
No, sir, I assumed they'd read the book.

617
00:37:03,953 --> 00:37:07,832
Well, how was the decision
to move Paul Settle arrived at?

618
00:37:07,833 --> 00:37:10,952
Mr Elkington called and said that
Mr Spearson had ordered Settle moved

619
00:37:10,953 --> 00:37:13,037
because he was acting up.

620
00:37:13,713 --> 00:37:15,992
Well, he was due to be released shortly.

621
00:37:15,993 --> 00:37:19,072
Like I said, sir, he wouldn't give an inch.

622
00:37:19,073 --> 00:37:21,552
And you said nothing more specific
than to move him?

623
00:37:21,553 --> 00:37:22,781
No, sir.

624
00:37:23,313 --> 00:37:25,032
Where are the rest of the prisoners
on the wing now?

625
00:37:25,033 --> 00:37:28,232
- In the workshops or classrooms, sir.
- All right.

626
00:37:28,233 --> 00:37:30,567
- Let me see the cell.
- Sir.

627
00:37:35,793 --> 00:37:36,862
Oh.

628
00:37:37,753 --> 00:37:39,392
Is this cell now reoccupied?

629
00:37:39,393 --> 00:37:42,392
Yes, we're back up to full capacity.

630
00:37:42,393 --> 00:37:44,472
Does that cause problems, Mr Spearson?

631
00:37:44,473 --> 00:37:47,232
It means there's no let-off, sir,
for staff or prisoners.

632
00:37:47,233 --> 00:37:49,567
Everyone's out now, working?

633
00:37:50,953 --> 00:37:54,352
Did Paul Settle join
in man activities, Mr Baggs?

634
00:37:54,353 --> 00:37:57,752
Yes, things were relaxed,
we didn't have a full house.

635
00:37:57,753 --> 00:38:02,503
Didn't you? Your colleague
Mr Elkington says that you did.

636
00:38:04,713 --> 00:38:08,672
Well, I suppose so,
if you count the damaged cells.

637
00:38:08,673 --> 00:38:10,232
Who's in the next cell?

638
00:38:10,233 --> 00:38:11,872
Stevie Quantro, sir.

639
00:38:11,873 --> 00:38:14,540
I'd like to question Mr Quantro.

640
00:38:14,713 --> 00:38:19,047
- What, now, sir?
- Yes. He'll need to take the oath.

641
00:38:21,353 --> 00:38:26,770
I told everyone already,
I didn't see nothing. How could I?

642
00:38:28,513 --> 00:38:33,513
I'm more interested in what
you might have heard, Mr Quantro.

643
00:38:33,833 --> 00:38:36,417
Nothing. I didn't hear nothing.

644
00:38:39,033 --> 00:38:42,033
Was there a disturbance on the wing?

645
00:38:42,753 --> 00:38:44,587
I didn't hear nothing.

646
00:38:45,953 --> 00:38:48,953
Well, what time did the alarm start?

647
00:38:51,473 --> 00:38:53,111
I don't remember.

648
00:38:56,153 --> 00:38:58,986
What time was Mr Settle found?

649
00:39:01,153 --> 00:39:03,403
I think it was at slop-out.

650
00:39:06,993 --> 00:39:11,112
- What did you make of Quantro?
- Oh, he seems very dodgy, Judge.

651
00:39:11,113 --> 00:39:13,232
Probably behaves like that
instinctively.

652
00:39:13,233 --> 00:39:15,312
He was scared of Spearson.

653
00:39:15,313 --> 00:39:18,672
Yeah. There's something going on
between Mr Spearson and the others,

654
00:39:18,673 --> 00:39:20,672
and the Assistant Governor, Baggs.

655
00:39:20,673 --> 00:39:24,832
Well, he won't stand up to anyone,
especially not Spearson.

656
00:39:24,833 --> 00:39:26,712
Get me the prison manifest
for August the 9th,

657
00:39:26,713 --> 00:39:29,632
let's find out exactly
who was in residence.

658
00:39:29,633 --> 00:39:32,232
The Prison Service will make
a song and dance about that.

659
00:39:32,233 --> 00:39:33,985
Fine, insist.

660
00:39:34,353 --> 00:39:38,112
And tell them I want the tapes from the
camera covering that corridor, as well.

661
00:39:38,113 --> 00:39:39,947
Ooh, this will be fun.

662
00:39:44,593 --> 00:39:49,927
Why does Deed want the manifests?
He's not conducting an inquiry.

663
00:39:54,393 --> 00:39:56,232
Well, he can't have them.

664
00:39:56,233 --> 00:40:00,067
Speak to Ian Rochester,
get him to talk to him.

665
00:40:00,233 --> 00:40:02,672
My lord, you had an open and shut case.

666
00:40:02,673 --> 00:40:05,552
There is not a shadow of doubt
that this man did this killing.

667
00:40:05,553 --> 00:40:07,592
No, what concerns me is ow he came to be in a position

668
00:40:07,593 --> 00:40:09,192
to do the killing in the first place.

669
00:40:09,193 --> 00:40:13,392
As bad as the system is,
we cannot have anarchy, or worse.

670
00:40:13,393 --> 00:40:14,912
- Or worse, my lord?
- Yes.

671
00:40:14,913 --> 00:40:19,992
Premeditation masquerading as anarchy.
I will have those manifests.

672
00:40:19,993 --> 00:40:24,632
And whatever the camera recorded
on that wing, if indeed anything.

673
00:40:24,633 --> 00:40:29,192
Judge, the Prison Service say the tape
from the night of August the 9th

674
00:40:29,193 --> 00:40:31,792
was misplaced after the inquiry.

675
00:40:31,793 --> 00:40:34,872
Well, that's dilator at best.

676
00:40:34,873 --> 00:40:36,872
At worst, the could be
covering something up.

677
00:40:36,873 --> 00:40:38,072
- Well, like what?
- Well, if they're not,

678
00:40:38,073 --> 00:40:39,832
why don't the just and over the record?

679
00:40:39,833 --> 00:40:41,599
The won't like their
authority undermined.

680
00:40:41,600 --> 00:40:43,472
The Prison Service
is a world of its own.

681
00:40:43,473 --> 00:40:45,557
Keep pressing them, Coop.

682
00:40:46,650 --> 00:40:50,889
You're not giving evidence
to ascertain guilty or innocence,

683
00:40:50,891 --> 00:40:52,570
you've already pleaded guilty.

684
00:40:52,571 --> 00:40:53,686
So...

685
00:40:54,531 --> 00:40:56,730
So, I get a light sentence?

686
00:40:56,731 --> 00:41:00,330
No, because you are in fact guilty.
ow, let's hear your story.

687
00:41:00,331 --> 00:41:03,330
With your leave, my lord, would you ear an application for Mrs Settle

688
00:41:03,331 --> 00:41:05,850
to return to court to hear
this examination?

689
00:41:05,851 --> 00:41:07,210
You know what she has to do.

690
00:41:07,211 --> 00:41:09,130
Mrs Settle just wants the truth.

691
00:41:09,131 --> 00:41:10,890
Yes, yes, yes, don't we all.

692
00:41:10,891 --> 00:41:14,330
She must apologise to the court and give
an undertaking not to interrupt again.

693
00:41:14,330 --> 00:41:15,649
Of course.

694
00:41:15,890 --> 00:41:20,049
What did you feel when
Mr Settle was put in your cell?

695
00:41:20,051 --> 00:41:21,643
Nothing, really.

696
00:41:22,851 --> 00:41:26,970
I didn't want him there.
The knew I didn't want him.

697
00:41:26,971 --> 00:41:28,890
How did Mr Settle react?

698
00:41:28,891 --> 00:41:33,610
Well, he didn't do nothing at first.
He said, did I want a bit of crack?

699
00:41:33,611 --> 00:41:35,890
- Crack cocaine?
- Yeah, plenty.

700
00:41:35,891 --> 00:41:37,650
What was your response?

701
00:41:37,651 --> 00:41:40,151
I called him a black scallwag!

702
00:41:44,451 --> 00:41:49,002
Tr not to do an,
not since my mum and brother OD'd.

703
00:41:51,011 --> 00:41:55,289
I asked Mr Spearson.
He helped me stay clean, he did.

704
00:41:57,891 --> 00:41:59,891
He didn't give up on me.

705
00:42:00,131 --> 00:42:01,715
What happened next?

706
00:42:03,731 --> 00:42:07,065
Black was scared.
He thought I'd pop him.

707
00:42:08,371 --> 00:42:10,871
That's why he offered me some.

708
00:42:11,491 --> 00:42:13,288
He kept having a go.

709
00:42:14,491 --> 00:42:16,610
Said I was scared to tr some.

710
00:42:16,611 --> 00:42:18,778
Did he show you his drugs?

711
00:42:19,771 --> 00:42:21,688
No, I didn't want none.

712
00:42:21,891 --> 00:42:24,970
So you don't actually know
that he had an drugs?

713
00:42:24,971 --> 00:42:30,138
It could have been a wind-up
so I'd pop him and he'd get moved.

714
00:42:32,371 --> 00:42:36,955
What happened when you didn't
respond to his drug offer?

715
00:42:37,451 --> 00:42:39,203
He kept having a go.

716
00:42:40,131 --> 00:42:44,881
Saying I couldn't do nothing
without Mr Spearson about me.

717
00:42:45,051 --> 00:42:48,801
He kept saying, what,
was I weak or something?

718
00:42:48,971 --> 00:42:50,927
I wasn't scared of him!

719
00:42:52,931 --> 00:42:54,523
Ask Mr Spearson.

720
00:42:55,451 --> 00:43:00,650
He said to me,
"You're not scared of no one on Earth."

721
00:43:00,851 --> 00:43:02,935
Did you attack Mr Settle?

722
00:43:03,051 --> 00:43:05,570
Yeah, I told you,
when he kept having a...

723
00:43:05,571 --> 00:43:07,821
When did you attack him?

724
00:43:09,531 --> 00:43:11,365
Just after lights out.

725
00:43:12,771 --> 00:43:15,021
Maybe an hour, or even two.

726
00:43:16,611 --> 00:43:21,528
I knew he was going to attack me, right?
The told me he was.

727
00:43:21,651 --> 00:43:26,850
But I was waiting,
scrunched up like a spring.

728
00:43:29,931 --> 00:43:33,181
Right? I kicked him. Right in the head!

729
00:43:33,891 --> 00:43:37,641
Well, he was hard.
He knew how to use himself.

730
00:43:37,811 --> 00:43:39,961
Got me around the throat.

731
00:43:40,171 --> 00:43:42,255
The warned me about that.

732
00:43:42,771 --> 00:43:45,730
Know he'd done a few with throat-holds.

733
00:43:45,731 --> 00:43:48,815
I done him in the back with me blade.

734
00:43:49,811 --> 00:43:52,478
I could hear the air coming out.

735
00:43:52,691 --> 00:43:55,728
It was like a tyre going down.

736
00:44:07,171 --> 00:44:09,338
Thank you, Mr Bradwell.

737
00:44:11,451 --> 00:44:13,009
Mr Mason Allen.

738
00:44:23,611 --> 00:44:29,129
Mr Bradwell, you have pleaded guilty
to manslaughter, haven't you?

739
00:44:31,771 --> 00:44:33,170
Haven't you?

740
00:44:34,571 --> 00:44:39,201
So you accept,
if you were defending yourself,

741
00:44:40,011 --> 00:44:45,085
the force used was not reasonable
but excessive,

742
00:44:45,891 --> 00:44:47,210
wasn't it?

743
00:44:47,491 --> 00:44:51,848
Furthermore, if it was
our own knife you used,

744
00:44:52,651 --> 00:44:56,401
how do you claim
this was self-defence at all?

745
00:45:02,731 --> 00:45:08,130
You planned to kill Mr Settle
at the first opportunity, didn't you?

746
00:45:08,131 --> 00:45:10,042
Hmm? Hmm?

747
00:45:10,531 --> 00:45:16,770
Seized your opportunity and then made up
the story about the drugs, didn't you?

748
00:45:16,771 --> 00:45:19,438
There were no drugs, were there?

749
00:45:19,731 --> 00:45:23,610
Mr Settle wasn't even
a known drug user, was he?

750
00:45:23,611 --> 00:45:29,527
In fact, he was known as
the choirboy, wasn't he?

751
00:45:31,731 --> 00:45:33,050
Wasn't he?

752
00:45:47,371 --> 00:45:53,455
Mr Bradwell, who was it that told you
to watch out for Mr Settle's attack?

753
00:45:53,771 --> 00:45:54,965
Mr Spearson.

754
00:45:56,291 --> 00:45:59,458
He warned me he was coming. The jig...

755
00:46:00,731 --> 00:46:02,290
You knew he was coming?

756
00:46:02,291 --> 00:46:03,730
Of course you didn't,
you didn't know.

757
00:46:03,731 --> 00:46:06,981
Mr Spearson, you've given your evidence.

758
00:46:09,651 --> 00:46:13,410
Who told you that you would be
getting Paul Settle in your cell?

759
00:46:13,411 --> 00:46:15,890
- Mr Spearson did.
- It's a lie, sir, he's mental.

760
00:46:15,891 --> 00:46:17,090
- Mr Spearson...
- He is, sir!

761
00:46:17,091 --> 00:46:18,610
You did. Mr Spearson...

762
00:46:18,611 --> 00:46:20,210
- Calm down, son!
- You said!

763
00:46:20,211 --> 00:46:21,485
Be quiet!

764
00:46:22,091 --> 00:46:26,341
It's a trial, not a chat show.
Bradwell, look at me.

765
00:46:32,531 --> 00:46:39,004
Why would Mr Spearson warn you that you
were getting Paul Settle in your cell?

766
00:46:39,331 --> 00:46:43,850
'Cause he knew I hated drug dealers.
Especially black ones.

767
00:46:43,851 --> 00:46:46,010
- Paul wouldn't touch drugs!
- Amen.

768
00:46:46,011 --> 00:46:47,490
I think we're a long way
from the truth here,

769
00:46:47,491 --> 00:46:48,650
but I will get somewhere close.

770
00:46:48,651 --> 00:46:51,901
Mr Bradwell, having killed Paul Settle,

771
00:46:52,531 --> 00:46:55,450
either because he provoked you
or because of self-defence,

772
00:46:55,451 --> 00:47:00,923
why did you then proceed to eat him?

773
00:47:08,571 --> 00:47:10,238
Tell the truth, Ben.

774
00:47:10,891 --> 00:47:17,391
Mr Spearson, don't you see enough of the
inside of a cell in your work? Shut up.

775
00:47:19,891 --> 00:47:21,290
Mr Bradwell?

776
00:47:23,491 --> 00:47:26,075
Mr Spearson's always helped me.

777
00:47:27,011 --> 00:47:28,763
He got me straight.

778
00:47:30,011 --> 00:47:31,330
I owe him.

779
00:47:32,011 --> 00:47:34,761
That's not what I asked you.

780
00:47:35,371 --> 00:47:37,538
Having killed Paul Settle,

781
00:47:39,931 --> 00:47:43,606
why did you decide to eat him?

782
00:47:57,891 --> 00:48:00,058
Take him back to the dock.

783
00:48:07,051 --> 00:48:10,885
Is the principal defence psychiatrist
in court?

784
00:48:12,371 --> 00:48:15,010
The prisoner is a
delusional schizophrenic.

785
00:48:15,011 --> 00:48:16,970
At times, he functions quite rationally.

786
00:48:16,971 --> 00:48:19,010
Is there a history to restate?

787
00:48:19,011 --> 00:48:20,845
Not one we penetrated.

788
00:48:21,011 --> 00:48:23,730
Other than he was
badly abused as a child,

789
00:48:23,731 --> 00:48:26,210
then again in various institutions.

790
00:48:26,211 --> 00:48:30,050
Did his mother and brother
die of drug overdoses?

791
00:48:30,051 --> 00:48:31,166
Yes.

792
00:48:31,651 --> 00:48:36,290
Mr Bradwell injected them with the drug,
thinking it was cut heroin.

793
00:48:36,291 --> 00:48:38,327
In fact, it was uncut.

794
00:48:38,971 --> 00:48:40,805
He was 11 at the time.

795
00:48:41,051 --> 00:48:45,530
He spent the next three days trying to
revive them before he was found.

796
00:48:45,531 --> 00:48:47,330
Doctor, in the time you spent
with Mr Bradwell,

797
00:48:47,331 --> 00:48:50,490
did you form an opinion as to whether he was capable of telling the truth?

798
00:48:50,491 --> 00:48:53,090
Very little credence should be given
to anything he says,

799
00:48:53,091 --> 00:48:54,970
unless forensically proven.

800
00:48:54,971 --> 00:48:58,638
Thank you, Doctor.
Mr Mason Allen? Thank you.

801
00:48:59,171 --> 00:49:03,687
Doctor, is he capable of understanding
right and wrong?

802
00:49:03,771 --> 00:49:08,810
He has a moral code. It's just
a bit off-kilter for the rest of us.

803
00:49:08,811 --> 00:49:09,880
Example?

804
00:49:09,971 --> 00:49:15,523
All non-Aryan races are inferior,
so it's acceptable to attack them.

805
00:49:15,611 --> 00:49:20,370
Did he understand what he was doing
at the time of the offense?

806
00:49:20,371 --> 00:49:21,850
Not in my opinion.

807
00:49:21,851 --> 00:49:23,290
Thank you, Doctor.

808
00:49:23,291 --> 00:49:27,450
Thank you, Doctor, you can step down.
You ma remain in court if you wish.

809
00:49:27,451 --> 00:49:31,930
Mrs Settle, would you be prepared
to give evidence under oath

810
00:49:31,931 --> 00:49:35,010
about your son's possible
involvement with drugs?

811
00:49:35,011 --> 00:49:38,930
Mrs Settle, I've taken this unusual step
in order to explore

812
00:49:38,931 --> 00:49:41,930
whether Paul, your son,
was involved with drugs.

813
00:49:41,931 --> 00:49:43,765
It's a lie. He wasn't.

814
00:49:43,771 --> 00:49:45,490
Neither selling nor taking?

815
00:49:45,491 --> 00:49:48,767
No, not Paul. He despised drugs.

816
00:49:48,851 --> 00:49:51,410
Because you see, that wasn't
Mr Bradwell's evidence.

817
00:49:51,411 --> 00:49:55,050
This is one big conspiracy.
He was put up to it.

818
00:49:55,051 --> 00:49:57,730
- What would be the object?
- I don't know.

819
00:49:57,731 --> 00:50:00,650
The racists in the Prison Service
are the real culprits.

820
00:50:00,651 --> 00:50:03,730
It's clear the internal inquiry
was unsatisfactory.

821
00:50:03,731 --> 00:50:05,650
He knew Paul was
being put into his cell.

822
00:50:05,651 --> 00:50:08,410
If we believe him over that statement,

823
00:50:08,411 --> 00:50:10,730
why not when he said that
Paul was selling drugs?

824
00:50:10,731 --> 00:50:13,010
Paul wouldn't, he just wouldn't.

825
00:50:13,011 --> 00:50:16,370
Or we could accept Dr Murra-West's
point of view,

826
00:50:16,371 --> 00:50:18,570
which is that Bradwell is
a delusional schizophrenic

827
00:50:18,571 --> 00:50:20,690
and that nothing he says
is to be believed.

828
00:50:20,691 --> 00:50:21,850
That might be the conclusion.

829
00:50:21,851 --> 00:50:24,050
Which neatly lets
the Prison Service off.

830
00:50:24,051 --> 00:50:27,250
Mr De Mel,
I am being fair to your client,

831
00:50:27,251 --> 00:50:31,251
and you have absolutely
no place in this hearing.

832
00:50:32,531 --> 00:50:36,690
Well, I can find no evidence of
Paul Settle's involvement with drugs,

833
00:50:36,691 --> 00:50:39,290
so I won't be pursuing
this point an further,

834
00:50:39,291 --> 00:50:42,890
nor, Mrs Mills, will I hold this point
against the defendant.

835
00:50:42,891 --> 00:50:46,770
Mrs Settle would like to make
an application for a judicial review.

836
00:50:46,771 --> 00:50:50,690
Twice the Attorney General has
refused her a private prosecution

837
00:50:50,691 --> 00:50:53,410
against the Prison Officers on duty
when Paul was killed.

838
00:50:53,411 --> 00:50:55,530
She must make an application
in the proper way.

839
00:50:55,531 --> 00:50:57,610
She has, but can't get anywhere.

840
00:50:57,611 --> 00:51:00,010
You said you wanted to be fair
to Mrs Settle,

841
00:51:00,011 --> 00:51:02,050
we won't get a better chance.

842
00:51:02,051 --> 00:51:05,530
I am sitting in the Crown Court,
Mr De Mel,

843
00:51:05,531 --> 00:51:07,370
as I'm sure even you have noticed.

844
00:51:07,371 --> 00:51:09,530
And even if you were granted
judicial review,

845
00:51:09,531 --> 00:51:11,410
you would still be up against
the Attorney General.

846
00:51:11,411 --> 00:51:13,490
They're scared of the truth!

847
00:51:13,491 --> 00:51:17,250
Well, a judicial review is unlikely
to reveal that, in an event.

848
00:51:17,251 --> 00:51:20,970
Bradwell said he knew that the
were putting Paul into his cell.

849
00:51:20,971 --> 00:51:27,305
Mrs Settle, I am trying the basis of
a manslaughter plea in a criminal court.

850
00:51:27,851 --> 00:51:31,330
Now, I will explain to you ow to apply for judicial review

851
00:51:31,331 --> 00:51:35,370
and I will help you
tr to get it if I can,

852
00:51:35,451 --> 00:51:38,650
but your evidence will have to be compelling.

853
00:51:38,651 --> 00:51:40,690
There's no doubt that this mother's
been given the run-around

854
00:51:40,691 --> 00:51:42,443
by the Attorney, Mont.

855
00:51:42,531 --> 00:51:45,490
- On behalf of the Home Office?
- Yeah, wouldn't be surprised.

856
00:51:45,491 --> 00:51:50,370
Send her my way, John, I'll get
my clerk to fast-track her hearing.

857
00:51:50,371 --> 00:51:52,450
You do know what you're
bringing down on your head?

858
00:51:52,451 --> 00:51:57,451
I have long since lost an expectation
of becoming a law lord.

859
00:51:57,571 --> 00:51:59,330
I can't guarantee
she'll get what she wants.

860
00:51:59,331 --> 00:52:02,530
I'm not sure that anything
will full satisfy.

861
00:52:02,531 --> 00:52:05,610
He is opening such a can of worms, Jo.

862
00:52:05,611 --> 00:52:07,690
What do you know, Newman?

863
00:52:07,691 --> 00:52:08,806
Jo?

864
00:52:10,371 --> 00:52:11,520
Marc?

865
00:52:12,531 --> 00:52:14,365
I'll see you in court.

866
00:52:17,171 --> 00:52:19,770
- It's so good to see you.
- Marc, what are you doing here?

867
00:52:19,771 --> 00:52:22,010
Well, I told you, applying for a job.

868
00:52:22,011 --> 00:52:24,250
Well, you didn't tell me
you were coming over so soon.

869
00:52:24,251 --> 00:52:25,850
Well, I start next week.

870
00:52:25,851 --> 00:52:27,690
Head of the Thoracic Department,
St James.

871
00:52:27,691 --> 00:52:30,010
- St James's, Brighton?
- Yes, I rented a house.

872
00:52:30,011 --> 00:52:31,930
Boys are off to boarding school.

873
00:52:31,931 --> 00:52:34,010
And Michael? Not South Africa?

874
00:52:34,011 --> 00:52:36,678
No, no, of course not. No, here.

875
00:52:38,931 --> 00:52:42,181
Why didn't you tell me you were coming?

876
00:52:42,251 --> 00:52:47,010
Well, I didn't want your reaction
to be part of my decision.

877
00:52:47,011 --> 00:52:48,080
Oh.

878
00:52:48,171 --> 00:52:51,322
No, no, I didn't mean it like that.

879
00:52:51,411 --> 00:52:55,370
Look, if I told you, said
I want you to be part of my life,

880
00:52:55,371 --> 00:52:58,130
I ma have decided not to come back
if you had said no.

881
00:52:58,131 --> 00:53:00,290
What if I would have said yes?

882
00:53:00,291 --> 00:53:03,050
I might have made the wrong decision
for the boys.

883
00:53:03,051 --> 00:53:06,170
Did you make the right decision
for everyone?

884
00:53:06,171 --> 00:53:07,570
Absolutely.

885
00:53:09,771 --> 00:53:13,366
You look wonderful.
Good to see you again.

886
00:53:14,291 --> 00:53:16,410
Do you want to go and get some tea?

887
00:53:16,411 --> 00:53:20,529
My palms are sweating,
so nervous.

888
00:53:21,011 --> 00:53:23,050
- I can't believe you're here.
- Hmm.

889
00:53:23,051 --> 00:53:24,564
Home Secretary.

890
00:53:24,651 --> 00:53:27,610
Deed seems determined to get
this woman her private prosecution.

891
00:53:27,611 --> 00:53:29,570
Ultimately, it's for
the Attorney General.

892
00:53:29,571 --> 00:53:32,155
With the bad press all the way.

893
00:53:32,371 --> 00:53:35,170
The Deputy Prime Minister sends is apologies, Sir Alan.

894
00:53:35,171 --> 00:53:37,370
He's delayed in a public inquiry.

895
00:53:37,371 --> 00:53:41,871
Thank you, Mrs Fox.
I'm sure we can manage without him.

896
00:53:45,611 --> 00:53:47,330
Perhaps the way to thwart them, Neil,

897
00:53:47,331 --> 00:53:49,770
is for me to allow
the private prosecution.

898
00:53:49,771 --> 00:53:51,210
Let the CPS take it over

899
00:53:51,211 --> 00:53:54,010
and find there's insufficient evidence
to proceed.

900
00:53:54,011 --> 00:53:55,810
Always assuming that's the case,
of course.

901
00:53:55,811 --> 00:53:58,723
And if there is evidence, Alan?

902
00:53:58,931 --> 00:54:01,970
The Prison Service gets dirt
chucked at it either way.

903
00:54:01,971 --> 00:54:03,570
Deed won't step away from this.

904
00:54:03,571 --> 00:54:06,155
Deed is a treacherous renegade.

905
00:54:06,211 --> 00:54:08,370
Perhaps you should agree
to a public inquiry.

906
00:54:08,371 --> 00:54:12,130
Neither you, the Director of the Prison
Service or the Cabinet would want that.

907
00:54:12,131 --> 00:54:16,090
The solution is certainly far worse
than the problem and very costly.

908
00:54:16,091 --> 00:54:17,450
Deed won't care about that.

909
00:54:17,451 --> 00:54:21,650
He feels all of government and
its offices should function openly.

910
00:54:21,651 --> 00:54:25,130
Then it's about time someone taught him
the facts of life about prison.

911
00:54:25,131 --> 00:54:26,970
With the ever-increasing tide
of blacks in your prison,

912
00:54:26,971 --> 00:54:29,805
we need to divide and manage them!

913
00:54:36,691 --> 00:54:39,525
- Then we're at Deed's mercy.
- No!

914
00:54:41,011 --> 00:54:43,525
Find a way to stop him.

915
00:54:44,931 --> 00:54:48,090
You've asked Mr Justice Everard
to hear a judicial review

916
00:54:48,091 --> 00:54:52,170
of the Attorney's refusal to allow a
private prosecution of prison officers.

917
00:54:52,171 --> 00:54:56,290
Should it succeed, the prisons
could become unmanageable.

918
00:54:56,291 --> 00:54:59,890
No, should it succeed and the prison
officers are successfully prosecuted,

919
00:54:59,891 --> 00:55:01,490
the management of the prisons

920
00:55:01,491 --> 00:55:04,130
would have to be urgently reviewed,
wouldn't it?

921
00:55:04,131 --> 00:55:05,490
Have you talked to Mont Everard?

922
00:55:05,491 --> 00:55:07,970
He said the process is in train, he refuses to budge.

923
00:55:07,971 --> 00:55:11,690
Well, then, fine.
Justice will be done, won't it?

924
00:55:11,691 --> 00:55:15,010
The Home Secretary has no solution
for the problems in prisons.

925
00:55:15,011 --> 00:55:18,890
Well, then, why is he so intent
on putting people in prison

926
00:55:18,891 --> 00:55:20,610
instead of looking for alternatives?

927
00:55:20,611 --> 00:55:22,690
He's answerable to
a volatile electorate.

928
00:55:22,691 --> 00:55:25,770
The sad fact is, John,
there is no solution.

929
00:55:25,771 --> 00:55:27,810
Well, if things are as bad
as you say the are, Ian,

930
00:55:27,811 --> 00:55:29,130
and I don't think for one moment

931
00:55:29,131 --> 00:55:31,210
that you would argue this
frivolously or lightly,

932
00:55:31,211 --> 00:55:33,890
then we must tear down
the entire edifice.

933
00:55:33,891 --> 00:55:35,450
- And replace it with what?
- Well, as long as we keep on

934
00:55:35,451 --> 00:55:37,130
shoring it up
and pretending it's all right,

935
00:55:37,131 --> 00:55:39,130
nothing's ever going to improve, is it?

936
00:55:39,131 --> 00:55:41,890
Meanwhile, what would you have done
with the likes of Bradwell?

937
00:55:41,891 --> 00:55:44,970
I know. I can't believe that
all those currently in prison

938
00:55:44,971 --> 00:55:47,360
are as wayward as Bradwell.

939
00:55:47,451 --> 00:55:51,170
Maybe it's time we started to look at
who we're putting inside.

940
00:55:51,171 --> 00:55:53,770
How he ever got Mont Everard
to entertain this rubbish!

941
00:55:53,771 --> 00:55:56,890
Well, there must be
some way to stop it?

942
00:55:56,891 --> 00:55:59,810
You are
the Home Secretary, after all.

943
00:55:59,811 --> 00:56:02,061
Yeah, kill Deed.

944
00:56:02,091 --> 00:56:04,090
He seems hell-bent
in wanting to help this woman

945
00:56:04,091 --> 00:56:05,330
achieve a private prosecution.

946
00:56:05,331 --> 00:56:08,970
If that happens, I ma as well and the prisons over to him as well.

947
00:56:08,971 --> 00:56:14,489
Neil, why don't you offer him
something he wants?

948
00:56:25,091 --> 00:56:29,758
Why would loverboy offer me
the prison manifests, George?

949
00:56:30,731 --> 00:56:32,530
Have the been doctored?

950
00:56:32,531 --> 00:56:35,698
- Oh, he assures me the haven't.
- Hmm.

951
00:56:35,891 --> 00:56:39,520
He wants something in return.

952
00:56:42,811 --> 00:56:45,890
This judicial review of
the Attorney's decision

953
00:56:45,891 --> 00:56:50,225
not to allow the private prosecution
is misconceived.

954
00:56:50,331 --> 00:56:55,166
He wants you to make that clear
to Mont Everard.

955
00:56:58,331 --> 00:57:00,748
Why are you offering me this?

956
00:57:01,451 --> 00:57:03,690
'Cause even if she was granted
the review,

957
00:57:03,691 --> 00:57:06,450
it's still not binding on the Attorney.

958
00:57:06,451 --> 00:57:09,868
Mrs Settle would go
straight to the media.

959
00:57:10,931 --> 00:57:14,265
The political fallout would be enormous.

960
00:57:17,491 --> 00:57:20,991
Have you got
the prison manifests with you?

961
00:57:28,051 --> 00:57:29,570
The evidence from prison staff

962
00:57:29,571 --> 00:57:31,690
was that the prison in general,
and A-wing in particular,

963
00:57:31,691 --> 00:57:33,443
was up to capacity.

964
00:57:33,851 --> 00:57:38,970
But according to this manifest,
there were 13 places overall spare,

965
00:57:38,971 --> 00:57:40,848
and three on A-wing.

966
00:57:40,931 --> 00:57:43,130
Yeah. What does that mean, Judge?

967
00:57:43,131 --> 00:57:47,010
Well, it could mean that Settle was put
in Bradwell's cell for a purpose.

968
00:57:47,011 --> 00:57:48,569
What purpose?

969
00:57:48,651 --> 00:57:51,250
When we find that out,
we might find out why he died.

970
00:57:51,251 --> 00:57:54,610
It won't
bring him back, though, Judge.

971
00:57:54,611 --> 00:57:56,010
That's all you feel his mother wants.

972
00:57:56,011 --> 00:57:58,610
It's like she's hammering away
at the reason

973
00:57:58,611 --> 00:58:03,611
to tr and avoid coming to terms
with the fact that he's dead.

974
00:58:04,731 --> 00:58:07,565
It must be very hard for a mother.

975
00:58:09,091 --> 00:58:10,729
Yeah, it is.

976
00:58:13,371 --> 00:58:15,770
Contact Mrs Channing,
tell her the deal's off

977
00:58:15,771 --> 00:58:17,410
if I don't get the tape
from that camera.

978
00:58:17,411 --> 00:58:19,578
She'll know what you mean.

979
00:58:20,451 --> 00:58:23,035
See now, according to Spearson,

980
00:58:23,091 --> 00:58:26,010
Stevie Quantro was in the cell
ext to Bradwell.

981
00:58:26,011 --> 00:58:28,330
Yes, Judge,
you interviewed him.

982
00:58:28,331 --> 00:58:30,490
Yes. But this manifest
suggests otherwise.

983
00:58:30,491 --> 00:58:34,241
It says that it was somebody
called Barr Page.

984
00:58:35,131 --> 00:58:38,450
Inform the Prison Service I want
Barr Page as my next witness in court.

985
00:58:38,451 --> 00:58:42,490
Okay. Um, Judge, you were left a message
from Sir Joseph Channing's clerk.

986
00:58:42,491 --> 00:58:44,890
She wondered if it was convenient
for you to meet today

987
00:58:44,891 --> 00:58:48,130
to review Michael Gilliat
in the drink-driving sentence.

988
00:58:48,131 --> 00:58:49,290
Is there time?

989
00:58:49,291 --> 00:58:53,930
That all depends how long the Prison
Service takes to produce Barr Page.

990
00:58:53,931 --> 00:58:56,570
They'd better produce him pretty quickly
then, haven't the?

991
00:58:56,571 --> 00:58:57,686
Okay.

992
00:59:02,051 --> 00:59:04,290
- Jo Mills.
- Jo, it's me. How are you?

993
00:59:04,291 --> 00:59:05,326
Pretty good.

994
00:59:05,411 --> 00:59:08,090
Good. Look, something's come up
in the Bradwell case.

995
00:59:08,091 --> 00:59:09,970
Do you fancy a walk with Mimi?

996
00:59:09,971 --> 00:59:11,370
I have some things to do.

997
00:59:11,371 --> 00:59:14,090
All things being equal, you could
be back in court in 10 minutes.

998
00:59:14,091 --> 00:59:16,370
There's a lot I can do
in 10 minutes.

999
00:59:16,371 --> 00:59:17,955
An of them with me?

1000
00:59:20,451 --> 00:59:22,570
I'll see you on the green.

1001
00:59:22,571 --> 00:59:23,686
Good.

1002
00:59:27,371 --> 00:59:29,282
Something happen, Jo?

1003
00:59:29,531 --> 00:59:31,490
I thought we were
talking about Bradwell.

1004
00:59:31,491 --> 00:59:35,810
Yeah, well, I want to talk about us.
I never see you.

1005
00:59:35,811 --> 00:59:39,395
- You've seen me in court.
- Oh, yes, I have.

1006
00:59:42,971 --> 00:59:44,370
John...

1007
00:59:47,611 --> 00:59:49,490
Are you going to honour your deal
with Neil Haughton?

1008
00:59:49,491 --> 00:59:51,004
We have dinner.

1009
00:59:52,491 --> 00:59:54,491
John, I'm very confused.

1010
00:59:55,211 --> 00:59:58,295
Yeah, well, now that makes two of us.

1011
01:00:00,611 --> 01:00:01,930
I'm sorry.

1012
01:00:10,891 --> 01:00:16,250
I trust we've all come to a conclusion
in the fate of Michael Gilliat?

1013
01:00:16,251 --> 01:00:17,889
Five years, my dear?

1014
01:00:17,971 --> 01:00:20,210
It's an appropriate sentence
for this offense.

1015
01:00:20,211 --> 01:00:22,330
With a 10-ear consecutive driving ban.

1016
01:00:22,331 --> 01:00:23,570
He must be banned from driving.

1017
01:00:23,571 --> 01:00:27,370
Well, it might be an appropriate
sentence, but is it radical?

1018
01:00:27,371 --> 01:00:29,730
Why not look for
some alternative to prison?

1019
01:00:29,731 --> 01:00:33,930
Hardly appropriate. I mean, there has
to be some sort of custodial sentence.

1020
01:00:33,931 --> 01:00:35,370
In view of the problems in the prisons,

1021
01:00:35,371 --> 01:00:37,410
I'm beginning to think that
some alternative is needed.

1022
01:00:37,411 --> 01:00:39,250
So what are the alternatives?

1023
01:00:39,251 --> 01:00:41,130
Fifty-day community punishment order?

1024
01:00:41,131 --> 01:00:43,570
Community service in a trauma ward
might be good.

1025
01:00:43,571 --> 01:00:45,290
- I was joking, John.
- Yeah, well.

1026
01:00:45,291 --> 01:00:47,566
You can't joke with John.

1027
01:00:47,651 --> 01:00:49,770
No, I'm with Joe.
It has to be custodial.

1028
01:00:49,771 --> 01:00:52,210
But I will concede to four years.

1029
01:00:52,211 --> 01:00:54,890
All right, let's give him
four years community service.

1030
01:00:54,891 --> 01:00:58,725
Four ear... 240 hours is the maximum.

1031
01:00:59,211 --> 01:01:03,210
- Yeah, I know, so let's change it.
- It's in statute, John, we can't.

1032
01:01:03,211 --> 01:01:04,690
Let's risk it.

1033
01:01:07,131 --> 01:01:08,290
It's worth a test.

1034
01:01:08,291 --> 01:01:10,170
It's not going to reassure
the dead boy's parents

1035
01:01:10,171 --> 01:01:13,050
that we have some concern for his life!

1036
01:01:13,051 --> 01:01:16,885
Let's tr to make
some kind of imaginative leap.

1037
01:01:17,651 --> 01:01:20,930
Why not suggest that he meets the victim's parents

1038
01:01:20,931 --> 01:01:24,015
and seek some sort of reconciliation?

1039
01:01:25,811 --> 01:01:28,930
The parents of the dead Bo
might consent.

1040
01:01:28,931 --> 01:01:31,890
Provided we stop Gilliat from getting
behind the wheel of a car

1041
01:01:31,891 --> 01:01:34,086
for a very long time.

1042
01:01:35,331 --> 01:01:39,570
These judges are getting
more and more ludicrous, Ian.

1043
01:01:39,571 --> 01:01:42,890
They're now trying to make a mocker
of criminal justice policy.

1044
01:01:42,891 --> 01:01:47,090
It might ease the burden on prisons
if it catches on.

1045
01:01:47,091 --> 01:01:49,370
I want wrong-doers where they belong.

1046
01:01:49,371 --> 01:01:52,050
Gilliat drove while drunk
and killed a young man.

1047
01:01:52,051 --> 01:01:55,650
This is Deed intent on embarrassing
the Home Office yet again.

1048
01:01:55,651 --> 01:01:58,210
We took the minds of some of
the other High Court judges,

1049
01:01:58,211 --> 01:02:00,490
some thought the move innovative.

1050
01:02:00,491 --> 01:02:03,410
They'd better enjoy their freedom
while the ma.

1051
01:02:03,411 --> 01:02:07,810
My new criminal justice bill
will create a statutory sentence

1052
01:02:07,811 --> 01:02:09,483
for ever offense.

1053
01:02:14,091 --> 01:02:17,930
The prisoner Barr Page has just been
brought from drug rehab in Durham Jail.

1054
01:02:17,931 --> 01:02:19,370
How long has he been there?

1055
01:02:19,371 --> 01:02:21,680
Since Paul Settle's death.

1056
01:02:21,771 --> 01:02:24,530
Did he give evidence
to the internal inquiry or not?

1057
01:02:24,531 --> 01:02:26,690
Because he's not on this list.

1058
01:02:26,691 --> 01:02:28,727
- Did the resist?
- No.

1059
01:02:28,811 --> 01:02:31,730
The prison officers who brought him
say he's a bit of a hard nut,

1060
01:02:31,731 --> 01:02:33,690
hasn't got a good word to say
about anybody.

1061
01:02:33,691 --> 01:02:36,108
Least of all judges.

1062
01:02:36,411 --> 01:02:38,995
Well, we'll find out, won't we?

1063
01:02:41,571 --> 01:02:44,410
I know what will happen
if I start talking.

1064
01:02:44,411 --> 01:02:46,970
I'm kicked off rehab
back onto the wings.

1065
01:02:46,971 --> 01:02:49,963
Is rehab a reward for not talking?

1066
01:02:50,251 --> 01:02:53,810
I didn't see nothing.
I didn't hear nothing.

1067
01:02:53,811 --> 01:02:58,930
Prison regimes, Mr Page, depend
upon the attitude of the inmates.

1068
01:02:58,931 --> 01:03:02,348
The harder you are,
the harder it becomes.

1069
01:03:02,371 --> 01:03:05,169
Yeah. I still didn't see nothing.

1070
01:03:05,571 --> 01:03:07,930
You were in the cell
ext to the defendant.

1071
01:03:07,931 --> 01:03:13,431
Did you hear them arguing on the night
that Paul Settle was killed?

1072
01:03:16,171 --> 01:03:21,090
I could guarantee that your place
on rehab was sacrosanct, you know.

1073
01:03:21,091 --> 01:03:22,683
- Yeah?
- Mmm.

1074
01:03:23,211 --> 01:03:25,770
How are you going to do that?
Be in the unit with me?

1075
01:03:25,771 --> 01:03:27,521
I have the authority.

1076
01:03:28,051 --> 01:03:33,250
Even if I did hear anything,
I wouldn't say it with screws here.

1077
01:03:33,251 --> 01:03:38,962
Okay. Messrs Baggs, Elkington,
Faker and Spearson. Remove ourselves.

1078
01:03:41,211 --> 01:03:45,628
Imagine having the power
of a judge, eh, Ben?

1079
01:03:52,931 --> 01:03:54,569
Well, Mr Page?

1080
01:03:54,651 --> 01:03:56,289
Nah.

1081
01:03:56,371 --> 01:03:58,327
I didn't hear no row.

1082
01:03:58,851 --> 01:04:02,610
Rehab for you seems to be a waste
of tax-payers' money, doesn't it?

1083
01:04:02,611 --> 01:04:04,861
Take him back to the cells.

1084
01:04:09,051 --> 01:04:10,850
Yeah, all right. You get what you want!

1085
01:04:10,851 --> 01:04:13,010
But I know what I'll get
when I get back inside!

1086
01:04:13,011 --> 01:04:14,761
No one will harm you.

1087
01:04:15,371 --> 01:04:17,650
How are you gonna stop it?
Be there with me?

1088
01:04:17,651 --> 01:04:19,090
Take him to the cells.

1089
01:04:19,091 --> 01:04:20,425
I heard the row.

1090
01:04:22,611 --> 01:04:24,611
The whole wing heard it.

1091
01:04:24,891 --> 01:04:26,225
Okay, come back.

1092
01:04:31,811 --> 01:04:33,311
What was it about?

1093
01:04:36,171 --> 01:04:39,610
Well, nutty there
didn't exactly like blacks.

1094
01:04:39,611 --> 01:04:41,278
I mean, look at him.

1095
01:04:42,051 --> 01:04:45,043
Yeah? You next.

1096
01:04:46,411 --> 01:04:48,288
Be quiet!

1097
01:04:51,411 --> 01:04:53,210
So, was the row about race?

1098
01:04:53,211 --> 01:04:54,883
Not what I heard.

1099
01:04:55,171 --> 01:04:58,505
It was about the usual.
Stuff going down.

1100
01:04:59,491 --> 01:05:01,610
It's the only thing
that makes your time bearable.

1101
01:05:01,611 --> 01:05:03,450
Putting your head somewhere else.

1102
01:05:03,451 --> 01:05:06,011
The were fighting about drugs?

1103
01:05:06,171 --> 01:05:08,421
That's all it's ever about.

1104
01:05:08,651 --> 01:05:12,210
We don't want to be two'd with
a schwartzer unless he's got some gear

1105
01:05:12,211 --> 01:05:15,045
and the choirboy always had plenty.

1106
01:05:16,491 --> 01:05:18,810
- He controlled the suppl.
- That's a lie.

1107
01:05:18,811 --> 01:05:22,210
Well, mother, if it is,
then it isn't doing me much good.

1108
01:05:22,211 --> 01:05:26,330
With your boy gone, life just got that
wee bit harder for everyone.

1109
01:05:26,331 --> 01:05:30,610
We all wanted to be in rehab then.
I got there soon after.

1110
01:05:30,611 --> 01:05:35,111
- Paul wouldn't!
- Mrs Settle, be silent or be removed.

1111
01:05:36,211 --> 01:05:40,568
In what way was the dead man
involved with drugs?

1112
01:05:41,691 --> 01:05:46,490
He was Mr I'm-gonna-make-you-happy.
If you had the means, he had the gear.

1113
01:05:46,491 --> 01:05:47,825
That's not true!

1114
01:05:47,891 --> 01:05:50,690
- Usher, remove Mrs Settle.
- This is entirely racially prejudicial.

1115
01:05:50,691 --> 01:05:52,363
Mr De Mel, too!

1116
01:06:03,011 --> 01:06:05,930
I'm a strung-out, who's HIV positive,

1117
01:06:05,931 --> 01:06:08,970
10 years still to do
and not too many prospects.

1118
01:06:08,971 --> 01:06:13,770
No one exactly wrote me a note,
but I know what's going on.

1119
01:06:13,771 --> 01:06:15,290
There's nothing else,

1120
01:06:15,291 --> 01:06:18,890
apart from the gear, the TV,
talk to the prisoners.

1121
01:06:18,891 --> 01:06:24,090
Mr Spearson, PO.
He hated the choirboy.

1122
01:06:25,091 --> 01:06:28,290
Well, he was black, for one thing,
and he was getting out.

1123
01:06:28,291 --> 01:06:32,523
Getting away clean.
See, the choirboy controlled it,

1124
01:06:32,611 --> 01:06:36,278
but didn't use it.
That's what he hated most.

1125
01:06:36,411 --> 01:06:39,911
Mr Spearson, his son died
on a crack floor.

1126
01:06:43,891 --> 01:06:46,490
He hated Settle getting away clean.

1127
01:06:46,491 --> 01:06:48,610
Did you know this, Mrs Mills?

1128
01:06:48,611 --> 01:06:49,760
No, my lord.

1129
01:06:51,091 --> 01:06:55,290
That's what Mr Spearson had
in common with nutty boy there.

1130
01:06:55,291 --> 01:06:59,610
The both lost someone to drugs.
He was able to use him.

1131
01:06:59,611 --> 01:07:01,010
Come on, Buzz.

1132
01:07:01,851 --> 01:07:03,370
You know the score.

1133
01:07:03,371 --> 01:07:05,250
Mr Spearson got me straight.

1134
01:07:05,251 --> 01:07:08,730
Yeah, Ben? How come he couldn't get me straight?

1135
01:07:08,731 --> 01:07:13,648
And all the other heads he wants supplied for a quiet life?

1136
01:07:15,131 --> 01:07:18,806
You're claiming
that the Principal Officer

1137
01:07:18,891 --> 01:07:20,930
was part of the drug culture?

1138
01:07:20,931 --> 01:07:24,250
You can't get that much dope inside
unless the are.

1139
01:07:24,251 --> 01:07:30,168
He wanted control to go with his man.
The choirboy was having none of it.

1140
01:07:31,411 --> 01:07:33,210
What time did the fight start?

1141
01:07:33,211 --> 01:07:36,010
About an hour and 10 minutes
after shutdown.

1142
01:07:36,011 --> 01:07:39,095
He brought the choirboy
to nutty's cell.

1143
01:07:39,451 --> 01:07:41,450
I had nothing else to do
but watch time

1144
01:07:41,451 --> 01:07:44,970
and sweat about
getting something the next day.

1145
01:07:44,971 --> 01:07:47,410
Paulie was shouting at them
to read the book.

1146
01:07:47,411 --> 01:07:50,330
"I can't go in with him. Read the book!"

1147
01:07:50,331 --> 01:07:52,570
That's what's written about him.

1148
01:07:52,571 --> 01:07:55,570
But little screw Elkington just said,

1149
01:07:55,571 --> 01:07:58,930
"It's time you learnt
some proper manners."

1150
01:07:58,931 --> 01:08:01,930
He was reminded
about Bradwell's record?

1151
01:08:01,931 --> 01:08:03,649
Choirboy told him.

1152
01:08:07,811 --> 01:08:11,730
Judge, the missing tape from the
CCTV camera in the prison just arrived.

1153
01:08:11,731 --> 01:08:12,981
Anything on it?

1154
01:08:13,011 --> 01:08:14,569
Oh, yes, Judge.

1155
01:08:16,651 --> 01:08:19,210
Thank you, Mr Page.
I'm adjourning for a short time.

1156
01:08:19,211 --> 01:08:20,690
There will be more questions.

1157
01:08:20,691 --> 01:08:22,930
Perhaps counsel would see me
in chambers.

1158
01:08:22,931 --> 01:08:24,265
All rise.

1159
01:08:26,611 --> 01:08:29,695
The internal inquiry didn't see this.

1160
01:08:30,771 --> 01:08:32,409
Time is 2:49.

1161
01:08:33,531 --> 01:08:37,290
The pathologist suggests
that Paul Settle is dead at this point,

1162
01:08:37,291 --> 01:08:38,804
but not eaten.

1163
01:08:41,131 --> 01:08:44,090
That doesn't put Spearson in
the time-frame for the killing, John.

1164
01:08:44,091 --> 01:08:46,605
No. Fast-forward a bit.

1165
01:08:52,011 --> 01:08:54,511
Nine minutes past three.

1166
01:08:55,451 --> 01:08:57,282
Spearson exits.

1167
01:08:57,731 --> 01:09:01,690
Now, doesn't it strike you as odd
that he doesn't raise the alarm?

1168
01:09:01,691 --> 01:09:03,124
Mr Spearson.

1169
01:09:04,771 --> 01:09:08,730
What was your duty roster
for the 24-hour period

1170
01:09:08,811 --> 01:09:11,279
covering the 9th of August?

1171
01:09:12,691 --> 01:09:15,050
I wasn't on duty that night, sir.

1172
01:09:15,051 --> 01:09:16,885
I didn't ask you that.

1173
01:09:18,011 --> 01:09:20,889
I was on duty
from 8am to 7pm.

1174
01:09:21,571 --> 01:09:24,370
Did you return to the prison at an time

1175
01:09:24,371 --> 01:09:28,330
during the night of the 9th
or the morning of the 10th?

1176
01:09:28,331 --> 01:09:29,525
No.

1177
01:09:29,611 --> 01:09:34,410
Not till I was called and told
that Settle's bod had been found, sir.

1178
01:09:34,411 --> 01:09:40,010
Then how do you account for the fact
that you were caught on the CCTV camera

1179
01:09:40,011 --> 01:09:44,095
in the small hours,
going into Mr Bradwell's cell?

1180
01:09:46,291 --> 01:09:48,625
It's not possible. I didn't.

1181
01:09:48,691 --> 01:09:51,941
Well, you're mistaken,
Mr Spearson.

1182
01:09:54,011 --> 01:09:56,047
There is no tape, sir.

1183
01:09:59,451 --> 01:10:00,804
Step down.

1184
01:10:10,211 --> 01:10:13,610
I'd like to question
Brian Elkington further.

1185
01:10:13,611 --> 01:10:16,370
Like I told the internal inquiry,
I don't look at the prisoners

1186
01:10:16,371 --> 01:10:19,210
until I make me rounds
just before slop-out.

1187
01:10:19,211 --> 01:10:24,690
You didn't check Mr Bradwell's cell
to see that Mr Settle was all right?

1188
01:10:24,691 --> 01:10:27,770
Ma have, I'm not sure.
There was a lot going on, sir.

1189
01:10:27,771 --> 01:10:31,010
When you looked in,
Mr Settle was alive and well?

1190
01:10:31,011 --> 01:10:32,649
Yes, he was fine.

1191
01:10:34,331 --> 01:10:39,883
He wasn't ling dead on the floor,
chewed by Bradwell?

1192
01:10:39,971 --> 01:10:42,388
No, of course not.

1193
01:10:43,891 --> 01:10:47,970
This is your last chance
to tell the truth, Mr Elkington.

1194
01:10:47,971 --> 01:10:50,531
I am, sir. I am.

1195
01:10:53,451 --> 01:10:54,679
Usher.

1196
01:11:00,091 --> 01:11:04,341
The time on the screen,
Mr Elkington, is 2:29.

1197
01:11:06,371 --> 01:11:08,538
Four hours after shutdown.

1198
01:11:15,291 --> 01:11:18,010
I must have made a mistake.
Settle was definitely alive.

1199
01:11:18,011 --> 01:11:21,610
No, the pathologist said that he died between 11:00 and 12:00.

1200
01:11:21,611 --> 01:11:24,603
By 2:29, he was certainly dead.

1201
01:11:24,691 --> 01:11:26,841
No. No, definitely not.

1202
01:11:26,931 --> 01:11:31,090
Mr Elkington, you are not on trial
for an crime here

1203
01:11:31,091 --> 01:11:35,930
and you are not obliged to answer an
questions that might incriminate you.

1204
01:11:35,931 --> 01:11:38,930
At the moment you might only be guilty
of dereliction of duty

1205
01:11:38,931 --> 01:11:41,431
for failing to report a death.

1206
01:11:45,371 --> 01:11:46,486
Okay.

1207
01:11:49,691 --> 01:11:51,890
I'll question Barr Page again.

1208
01:11:51,891 --> 01:11:53,165
He was dead!

1209
01:11:55,091 --> 01:11:59,250
I was too scared to go into the cell
with that nutter.

1210
01:11:59,251 --> 01:12:01,003
I told Mr Spearson!

1211
01:12:01,411 --> 01:12:04,730
He said he'd deal with it.
I mean, he was the Principal Officer.

1212
01:12:04,731 --> 01:12:07,130
He said he'd tell Mr Baggs,
the Assistant Governor.

1213
01:12:07,131 --> 01:12:10,362
I did my duty! I told the PO!

1214
01:12:12,171 --> 01:12:18,671
I would suggest that you urgently seek
the advice of a solicitor, Mr Elkington.

1215
01:12:22,082 --> 01:12:27,332
What time did the fight between
Mr Bradwell and Paul Settle end?

1216
01:12:28,442 --> 01:12:33,192
About 10 past 11.
Only lasted about three minutes at most.

1217
01:12:34,282 --> 01:12:36,601
I listened, but nothing else happened.

1218
01:12:36,602 --> 01:12:41,881
Nutty Boy there was laughing,
saying, "You're just so dumb, Amos."

1219
01:12:43,442 --> 01:12:45,194
Then it went silent.

1220
01:12:45,922 --> 01:12:47,801
Choirboy didn't say nothing.

1221
01:12:47,802 --> 01:12:50,801
Is that the last thing that happened until the bod was discovered?

1222
01:12:50,802 --> 01:12:55,969
No. About 10 to three, Mr Spearson came
and opened up the cell.

1223
01:12:56,282 --> 01:12:58,721
And how do you know who it was?

1224
01:12:58,722 --> 01:13:00,639
I recognised his voice.

1225
01:13:02,442 --> 01:13:06,276
First, I'd drifted off.
Then something woke me.

1226
01:13:07,762 --> 01:13:12,346
The sound of Ben Bradwell crying,
blubbering like a baby.

1227
01:13:13,682 --> 01:13:17,266
I couldn't hear
what Mr Spearson was saying.

1228
01:13:17,602 --> 01:13:20,936
But when I did,
I thought I was tripping.

1229
01:13:21,682 --> 01:13:25,016
Nutty there was saying he wouldn't do it,

1230
01:13:25,202 --> 01:13:28,202
begging Mr Spearson not to make him.

1231
01:13:30,122 --> 01:13:31,801
Make him do what?

1232
01:13:34,842 --> 01:13:39,926
Mr Spearson was telling him
to bite chunks out of the choirboy.

1233
01:13:40,322 --> 01:13:43,072
Kept saying it would get him off.

1234
01:13:44,202 --> 01:13:48,369
I definitely thought it was
some sort of nightmare.

1235
01:13:49,162 --> 01:13:53,761
I could hear nutty there, choking,
as Mr Spearson's saying,

1236
01:13:53,762 --> 01:13:58,929
"More, you coward. It was him
that killed your mum and brother."

1237
01:13:59,722 --> 01:14:02,139
It lasted about half an hour.

1238
01:14:03,242 --> 01:14:05,659
All the while he was choking.

1239
01:14:06,762 --> 01:14:08,639
Then Mr Spearson left.

1240
01:14:11,122 --> 01:14:16,372
After that, things went silent
until the found him next morning.

1241
01:14:25,682 --> 01:14:27,877
Mr Bradwell,

1242
01:14:27,962 --> 01:14:33,629
what did you feel when you bit
into the flesh of another human being?

1243
01:14:44,162 --> 01:14:45,800
It was horrible.

1244
01:14:47,242 --> 01:14:48,880
Like raw steak.

1245
01:14:51,122 --> 01:14:53,289
I had to bite really hard.

1246
01:15:03,642 --> 01:15:05,234
Mrs Mills, um...

1247
01:15:06,482 --> 01:15:09,899
do you have an questions
for this witness?

1248
01:15:11,722 --> 01:15:16,639
Mr Page, where was Assistant Governor
Baggs during all this?

1249
01:15:17,122 --> 01:15:19,372
He did as Mr Spearson said.

1250
01:15:20,642 --> 01:15:24,561
Didn't come up to the wing
until Mr Spearson told him to,

1251
01:15:24,562 --> 01:15:26,562
after the alarm went up.

1252
01:15:30,242 --> 01:15:31,436
Mr...

1253
01:15:41,162 --> 01:15:44,681
For the first time in my career,
my lord, I am lost

1254
01:15:44,682 --> 01:15:48,266
as to how I can be
of an further assistance.

1255
01:15:48,882 --> 01:15:54,832
Then I'll adjourn
and consider my findings till morning.

1256
01:15:55,282 --> 01:15:56,616
All rise.

1257
01:15:57,522 --> 01:16:01,961
I don't want you talking to this judge.
I don't want you saying you were wrong.

1258
01:16:01,962 --> 01:16:03,879
The press will kill us.

1259
01:16:04,522 --> 01:16:08,201
They’ll kill you, maybe.
All I ever wanted was the truth.

1260
01:16:08,202 --> 01:16:09,476
Me, too.

1261
01:16:14,882 --> 01:16:18,441
Mrs Settle asked if she could have a word, Judge.

1262
01:16:18,442 --> 01:16:20,921
- With her mouthpiece?
- No, on her own.

1263
01:16:20,922 --> 01:16:22,041
Oh. All right.

1264
01:16:22,042 --> 01:16:27,209
See if you can catch counsel, will you?
Ask them to come along.

1265
01:16:27,922 --> 01:16:29,361
Mrs Mills, the judge...

1266
01:16:29,362 --> 01:16:33,321
I'm sorry, Coop,
I've got a terribly important date.

1267
01:16:33,322 --> 01:16:34,471
Mrs Settle.

1268
01:16:36,922 --> 01:16:39,521
I wanted to set
the record straight, sir.

1269
01:16:39,522 --> 01:16:42,041
It looks as if I was ling
to protect my son.

1270
01:16:42,042 --> 01:16:43,481
Mothers are inclined to...

1271
01:16:43,482 --> 01:16:47,732
I didn't know Paul was
involved with drugs, I swear.

1272
01:16:48,882 --> 01:16:50,474
It's not proven.

1273
01:16:51,162 --> 01:16:56,441
We only have the evidence of two
perhaps not-very-reliable witnesses.

1274
01:16:56,442 --> 01:16:58,776
The both said the same, sir.

1275
01:16:59,402 --> 01:17:00,517
Yes.

1276
01:17:03,442 --> 01:17:09,692
Mrs Settle, would you consider
not pursuing your private prosecution?

1277
01:17:10,282 --> 01:17:12,881
There is more to Paul's death
than they're saying.

1278
01:17:12,882 --> 01:17:14,235
Yeah, possibly.

1279
01:17:15,082 --> 01:17:18,241
But you see, what's likely to happen,
should you succeed,

1280
01:17:18,242 --> 01:17:22,281
is that the CPS will take it over.

1281
01:17:22,962 --> 01:17:25,841
Then the will decide
that there is insufficient evidence

1282
01:17:25,842 --> 01:17:27,721
to proceed against these men.

1283
01:17:27,722 --> 01:17:29,599
That's not right, sir.

1284
01:17:30,562 --> 01:17:31,961
The can't.

1285
01:17:33,522 --> 01:17:36,561
No, well, unfortunately,
we live under a legal system

1286
01:17:36,562 --> 01:17:38,961
that doesn't promote
individual freedoms,

1287
01:17:38,962 --> 01:17:41,796
individual expressions of justice.

1288
01:17:44,682 --> 01:17:47,355
I just wanted justice for Paul.

1289
01:17:49,842 --> 01:17:51,673
The did kill my boy.

1290
01:17:54,762 --> 01:17:56,846
I'm just being practical.

1291
01:17:57,522 --> 01:17:58,921
I am sorry.

1292
01:18:05,489 --> 01:18:08,048
Mrs Settle's backed off er private prosecution.

1293
01:18:08,049 --> 01:18:10,328
Oh. That's wonderful, darling.

1294
01:18:10,329 --> 01:18:13,728
Do you think Deed's accepted
the facts of life about your prisons?

1295
01:18:13,729 --> 01:18:17,005
Uh, he always was a pragmatist.

1296
01:18:17,089 --> 01:18:19,842
Yeah. Everything has its price.

1297
01:18:28,649 --> 01:18:30,168
Hello. This is Jo Mills.

1298
01:18:30,169 --> 01:18:33,888
Please leave a message
and I'll call you back. Thanks.

1299
01:18:33,889 --> 01:18:35,686
Um, Jo, it's me.

1300
01:18:35,769 --> 01:18:40,648
I thought you might want to hear what
I'm going to do with Bradwell, um...

1301
01:18:40,649 --> 01:18:43,066
I'll tell you in the morning.

1302
01:18:59,249 --> 01:19:00,318
He.

1303
01:19:02,169 --> 01:19:03,966
Oh. Come on in.

1304
01:19:04,049 --> 01:19:07,049
Where is he? Will he let me hug him?

1305
01:19:16,529 --> 01:19:17,803
Michael?

1306
01:19:18,929 --> 01:19:21,263
I can't believe it.

1307
01:19:21,289 --> 01:19:25,248
You've grown, and you look so... cool.

1308
01:19:25,969 --> 01:19:27,768
You didn't tell me
you were coming back.

1309
01:19:27,769 --> 01:19:29,168
It was a secret.

1310
01:19:29,249 --> 01:19:34,333
No. A surprise, Mike.
Well, that's what his brothers call him.

1311
01:19:34,489 --> 01:19:36,047
Where are the?

1312
01:19:36,129 --> 01:19:39,968
Oh, term started. But your school
doesn't start until tomorrow.

1313
01:19:39,969 --> 01:19:41,528
Tomorrow? But then I'll hardly see you.

1314
01:19:41,529 --> 01:19:43,568
Well, there'll always be the holidays.

1315
01:19:43,569 --> 01:19:46,168
Well, Marc, does he have to go?
Can't he stay with me for a bit?

1316
01:19:46,169 --> 01:19:47,768
My term starts tomorrow.

1317
01:19:47,769 --> 01:19:49,805
Jo, I'm sorry.

1318
01:19:50,809 --> 01:19:54,248
I've got to rip my department apart
and put it back together again

1319
01:19:54,249 --> 01:19:57,968
in record time,
or else we can lose a lot of funding.

1320
01:19:57,969 --> 01:20:00,969
Oh, well. At least I've got tonight.

1321
01:20:05,729 --> 01:20:08,846
Judge! Judge!

1322
01:20:09,529 --> 01:20:11,368
The prisoner Barry Page is dead.

1323
01:20:11,369 --> 01:20:13,564
What? Oh. How? When?

1324
01:20:13,649 --> 01:20:16,649
He died of a massive drugs overdose.

1325
01:20:18,609 --> 01:20:20,768
I promised him protection.

1326
01:20:20,769 --> 01:20:23,019
He was in the local prison.

1327
01:20:25,089 --> 01:20:28,768
He was the key witness.
It means the get away with it again.

1328
01:20:28,769 --> 01:20:30,202
All rise.

1329
01:20:46,249 --> 01:20:49,368
Having examined the evidence
and read all the psychiatric reports

1330
01:20:49,369 --> 01:20:52,953
on Mr Bradwell,
I have concluded as follows.

1331
01:20:54,249 --> 01:20:57,168
The appropriate way
to sentence this case is as one

1332
01:20:57,169 --> 01:21:01,408
in which the defendant was suffering
from such an abnormality of mind

1333
01:21:01,409 --> 01:21:03,488
as diminished his responsibility

1334
01:21:03,489 --> 01:21:06,728
for what would otherwise have been murder.

1335
01:21:06,729 --> 01:21:08,648
Would you hear
a plea in mitigation, my lord?

1336
01:21:08,649 --> 01:21:11,368
Are you going to tell me
what a victim he is?

1337
01:21:11,369 --> 01:21:15,528
- There can be no doubt...
- No, no. I agree, Mrs Mills.

1338
01:21:15,529 --> 01:21:17,648
What would you have me do with him?

1339
01:21:17,649 --> 01:21:18,928
Place him not back in prison,

1340
01:21:18,929 --> 01:21:22,528
but in a unit where he might get some treatment.

1341
01:21:22,529 --> 01:21:24,247
Oh. Thank you.

1342
01:21:24,329 --> 01:21:28,448
I would like him to spend
the rest of his days in Africa,

1343
01:21:28,449 --> 01:21:30,368
looking after AIDS victims.

1344
01:21:30,369 --> 01:21:32,368
But that's not within my power

1345
01:21:32,369 --> 01:21:35,928
and I'm sure those poor, wretched people
are suffering enough already.

1346
01:21:35,929 --> 01:21:37,848
So, thank you, Mrs Mills.

1347
01:21:37,849 --> 01:21:39,849
- My lord...
- Thank you.

1348
01:21:42,489 --> 01:21:48,573
Mr Bradwell, I am not going to send you
back to prison. Do you understand?

1349
01:21:51,769 --> 01:21:54,008
Mr Spearson said I'd get a result.

1350
01:21:54,009 --> 01:21:55,843
Yes, yes, yes. Sit down.

1351
01:21:57,289 --> 01:22:00,928
I am satisfied
that you are still mentally ill

1352
01:22:00,929 --> 01:22:04,168
and I propose, bearing in mind
the evidence of psychiatrists,

1353
01:22:04,169 --> 01:22:07,088
to order your detention
in a special hospital

1354
01:22:07,089 --> 01:22:09,728
with a further order
restricting your release.

1355
01:22:09,729 --> 01:22:12,608
Mrs Mills will explain to you
what this means.

1356
01:22:12,609 --> 01:22:15,448
Please remain where you are
for the moment.

1357
01:22:15,449 --> 01:22:16,802
Now, then.

1358
01:22:20,449 --> 01:22:25,921
Would Messrs Baggs, Elkington,
Faker and Spearson stand up?

1359
01:22:31,089 --> 01:22:34,448
You're probably aware by now
that Mrs Settle has decided

1360
01:22:34,449 --> 01:22:37,616
not to pursue her private prosecution.

1361
01:22:38,009 --> 01:22:39,727
You will also know

1362
01:22:41,129 --> 01:22:44,546
that the main witness
against you is dead.

1363
01:22:45,609 --> 01:22:49,608
You've escaped justice once through
a dilator internal prison inquiry.

1364
01:22:49,609 --> 01:22:51,648
It's not going to happen again.

1365
01:22:51,649 --> 01:22:54,482
Despite the death of Barry Page,

1366
01:22:54,569 --> 01:22:58,208
I'm sending a report to
the Director of Public Prosecutions,

1367
01:22:58,209 --> 01:23:01,768
urging him to revisit the prosecution
of each one of you

1368
01:23:01,769 --> 01:23:04,408
for aiding and abetting
the murder of Paul Settle,

1369
01:23:04,409 --> 01:23:08,688
for criminal conspiracy and for
perverting the course of justice.

1370
01:23:08,689 --> 01:23:14,446
There is a prima facie case
against each one of you.

1371
01:23:16,289 --> 01:23:17,688
Furthermore,

1372
01:23:18,969 --> 01:23:22,359
for their safety, I'm going to recommend

1373
01:23:22,449 --> 01:23:24,528
that the Director General
of the Prison Services

1374
01:23:24,529 --> 01:23:27,808
moves all the prisoners
who were on A-block

1375
01:23:27,809 --> 01:23:30,608
at the time of Paul Settle's death.

1376
01:23:30,609 --> 01:23:33,609
I want them placed in a single unit.

1377
01:23:33,849 --> 01:23:38,286
I do not want a recurrence

1378
01:23:38,369 --> 01:23:41,645
of what happened to Barr Page.

1379
01:23:46,529 --> 01:23:47,757
Get out.

1380
01:24:01,809 --> 01:24:03,143
All rise.

1381
01:24:11,929 --> 01:24:14,608
Did you manage to contact
Mr and Mrs Foreman, Coop?

1382
01:24:14,609 --> 01:24:18,288
Yes, Judge. The didn't want to meet
with you, but the did agree to.

1383
01:24:18,289 --> 01:24:19,404
Good.

1384
01:24:24,129 --> 01:24:27,041
John! Can I have a word?

1385
01:24:27,129 --> 01:24:31,248
Uh, I'm racing to a meeting in London.
Do you want to talk on the way?

1386
01:24:31,249 --> 01:24:34,648
No, there's someone I have to see
down here. It'll keep.

1387
01:24:34,649 --> 01:24:36,608
- Jo...
- I thought it was really positive,

1388
01:24:36,609 --> 01:24:38,943
what you did in court today.

1389
01:24:39,609 --> 01:24:41,408
Well, the Home Secretary doesn't.

1390
01:24:41,409 --> 01:24:45,488
According to George, he thinks I'm
the most dangerous judge in the country.

1391
01:24:45,489 --> 01:24:47,656
- What a compliment.
- Yes.

1392
01:25:00,569 --> 01:25:04,357
A young man got drunk, got into his car

1393
01:25:04,449 --> 01:25:07,247
and killed your 17-ear-old son.

1394
01:25:07,329 --> 01:25:11,608
Then, on appeal, three judges who had
nothing to do with the original case,

1395
01:25:11,609 --> 01:25:15,528
replaced the original prison sentence
with a non-custodial one.

1396
01:25:15,529 --> 01:25:19,728
The Daily Mail described you
as "righteously angry".

1397
01:25:19,729 --> 01:25:23,728
You're disrespecting Peter's life.
You're putting so little value on it.

1398
01:25:23,729 --> 01:25:26,168
If we could deliver up
Michael Gilliat to you

1399
01:25:26,169 --> 01:25:28,888
to end his life, would you do it?

1400
01:25:28,889 --> 01:25:31,642
No, of course not.

1401
01:25:33,529 --> 01:25:38,048
We've never supported
capital punishment, have we, Paul?

1402
01:25:38,049 --> 01:25:40,966
No, we just want justice for Peter.

1403
01:25:41,849 --> 01:25:44,016
What sort of boy was Peter?

1404
01:25:45,849 --> 01:25:47,043
Oh...

1405
01:25:49,129 --> 01:25:52,879
Well, he wasn't funny or clever,
was he, Paul?

1406
01:25:54,489 --> 01:25:58,084
He was a bit awkward at times,
even clumsy.

1407
01:25:59,289 --> 01:26:01,873
But we loved him just the same.

1408
01:26:04,249 --> 01:26:09,166
He was very good at computers.
Well, that's how he met Kylie.

1409
01:26:10,809 --> 01:26:13,643
She was his very first girlfriend.

1410
01:26:14,969 --> 01:26:18,208
Oh, he was so excited when
she said she'd go out with him.

1411
01:26:18,209 --> 01:26:22,168
He could barely get himself dressed,
could he, Paul?

1412
01:26:22,169 --> 01:26:25,445
My two colleagues were not very keen

1413
01:26:25,529 --> 01:26:28,408
to remove the custodial element
from Mr Gilliat's sentence.

1414
01:26:28,409 --> 01:26:30,568
It was me that persuaded them.

1415
01:26:30,569 --> 01:26:33,448
In fairness, John,
we were prepared to reduce it.

1416
01:26:33,449 --> 01:26:37,761
Gilliat shouldn't have
got into a car drunk.

1417
01:26:37,849 --> 01:26:40,688
- He's a menace!
- Nobody disputes that,

1418
01:26:40,689 --> 01:26:44,248
or that he should never be allowed
to drive a car again.

1419
01:26:44,249 --> 01:26:47,088
But no matter how long
Gilliat spends in prison,

1420
01:26:47,089 --> 01:26:51,480
nothing is going to restore
our son to you, is it?

1421
01:26:52,449 --> 01:26:55,116
Well, what do you want us to do?

1422
01:26:55,529 --> 01:26:57,281
Forgive and forget?

1423
01:26:58,369 --> 01:27:00,328
Pretend that he didn't kill Peter?

1424
01:27:00,329 --> 01:27:04,608
No, I don't want you to forget.
I don't want either of you to forget.

1425
01:27:04,609 --> 01:27:07,526
But I don't want Gilliat to forget.

1426
01:27:08,809 --> 01:27:10,401
Forgive? Well...

1427
01:27:13,409 --> 01:27:16,088
Forgiveness,
along with compassion,

1428
01:27:16,089 --> 01:27:20,839
is perhaps the greatest gift
that human beings can bestow.

1429
01:27:21,369 --> 01:27:25,536
I'm not saying that
we can do it lightly or easily,

1430
01:27:26,049 --> 01:27:28,688
but if we can find it within ourselves

1431
01:27:28,689 --> 01:27:32,045
then, perhaps, it can often set us free.

1432
01:27:32,449 --> 01:27:34,808
What Sir John is hoping is that

1433
01:27:34,809 --> 01:27:40,893
you’d agree to meet with Michael Gilliat
as a first step to reconciliation.

1434
01:27:41,609 --> 01:27:44,928
I can see no other way
to help you unburden

1435
01:27:44,929 --> 01:27:46,848
this terrible suffering
that you're carrying.

1436
01:27:46,849 --> 01:27:49,079
It's too much to bear.

1437
01:27:49,169 --> 01:27:53,919
If I could help you to bear it,
then I would, but I can't.

1438
01:27:57,889 --> 01:28:00,556
But perhaps Michael Gilliat can.

